#edb/ref 17592186272329 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186059827 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272276 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186059827 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272277 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186059827 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272278 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186059827 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272279 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186059827 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272280 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186059827 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272337 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186060050 1068 #edb/ref 17592186056628 13194139533402 17592186045585 false] [17592186060050 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271853 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186060050 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186060050 1071 "The vector $a$ has magnitude $9$, the vector $b$ has magnitude $5$, the angle between $a$ and $b$ is $140^{∘}$ and $n$ is the unit vector that points in the direction of $a \\cdot b$. Find $a \\cdot b$ in terms of $n$. Round your final answer to the nearest integer where appropriate.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139533402 17592186045585 false] [17592186060050 1071 "The vector $a$ has magnitude $9,$ the vector $b$ has magnitude $5,$ the angle between $a$ and $b$ is ${140}^{∘}$ and $n$ is the unit vector that points in the direction of $a\\times b.$ Find $a\\times b$ in terms of $n.$ Round your final answer to the nearest integer where appropriate." 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186060050 1074 "We use the formula for the cross product and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\times b & =|a|\\cdot |b|\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot n \\\\  & =9\\cdot 5\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{140}^{∘}\\cdot n \\\\  & =45\\cdot (0.642…)\\cdot n \\\\  & =(28.925…)\\cdot n \\\\  & ≈29n.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186060051 1068 #edb/ref 17592186056634 13194139533402 17592186045585 false] [17592186060051 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271898 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186060051 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186060051 1071 "The vector $u$ has magnitude $6$, the vector $v$ has magnitude $7$, the angle between $u$ and $v$ is $134^{∘}$ and $n$ is the unit vector that points in the direction of $u \\cdot v$. Find $u \\cdot v$ in terms of $n$. Round your final answer to one decimal place where appropriate.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139533402 17592186045585 false] [17592186060051 1071 "The vector $u$ has magnitude $6$, the vector $v$ has magnitude $7,$ the angle between $u$ and $v$ is ${134}^{∘}$ and $n$ is the unit vector that points in the direction of $u\\times v.$ Find $u\\times v$ in terms of $n.$ Round your final answer to one decimal place where appropriate." 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186060051 1074 "We use the formula for the cross product and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\times v & =|u|\\cdot |v|\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot n \\\\  & =6\\cdot 7\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{134}^{∘}\\cdot n \\\\  & =42\\cdot (0.719\\,33…)\\cdot n \\\\  & =(30.212\\,27…)\\cdot n \\\\  & ≈30.2n.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186064291 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272321 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186064291 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272322 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186064291 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272324 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186064318 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272312 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186064318 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272313 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186064319 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272330 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186064319 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272331 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186064319 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272332 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186064319 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272333 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186064319 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272334 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186064509 1068 #edb/ref 17592186061254 13194139533402 17592186045585 false] [17592186064509 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271928 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186064509 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186064509 1074 "The concavity of a function refers to the direction in which its graph curves.\n\n- If the graph curves upward, like a \"smile\" ($⌣$), then the graph is said to be *concave up*.\n\n- If the graph curves downward, like a \"frown\" ($⌢$), then the graph is said to be *concave down*.\n\nIt's helpful to remember the mnemonic \"concave down like a frown.\"\n\nOnly graph III curves upward (like a \"smile\") among the given graphs." 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186068830 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272209 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186068833 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272314 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186068833 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272315 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186068833 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272326 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186068833 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272327 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186068847 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272352 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186068847 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272359 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186068850 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272351 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186068879 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272298 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186068880 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272048 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186068880 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272049 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186068880 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272050 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186068880 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272051 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186068881 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272302 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186068881 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272305 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186068882 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272309 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186068882 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272310 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186069064 1068 #edb/ref 17592186065787 13194139533402 17592186045585 false] [17592186069064 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271832 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186069064 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186069064 1071 "Let $x_{1}$ and $x_{2}$ be the solutions of the equation $x^{2} - 2x = 24$. Find the value $x_{1}^{2} + x_{2}^{2}$.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $x^{2} - 2x + 1$ is a perfect square.*" 13194139533402 17592186045585 false] [17592186069064 1071 "Let ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ be the solutions of the equation ${x}^{2}-2x=24.$ Find the value ${x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}.$ \n\n*Hint: Use the fact that ${x}^{2}-2x+1$ is a perfect square.*" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186069064 1074 "Using the hint, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2x+1=(x-1{)}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we complete the square on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-2x & =24 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2\\cdot 1\\cdot x & =24 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2\\cdot 1\\cdot x+{1}^{2}-{1}^{2} & =24 \\\\ (x-1{)}^{2}-1 & =24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we isolate the perfect square by adding $1$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1{)}^{2}-1+1 & =24+1 \\\\ (x-1{)}^{2} & =25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1{)}^{2} & =25 \\\\ \\sqrt{(x-1{)}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{25} \\\\ |x-1| & =5 \\\\ x-1 & =\\pm 5 \\\\ x & =1\\pm 5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify the two solutions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =1-5=-4 \\\\ {x}_{2} & =1+5=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}=(-4{)}^{2}+{6}^{2}=52.\n$$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186069067 1068 #edb/ref 17592186065805 13194139533402 17592186045585 false] [17592186069067 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271729 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186069067 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186069067 1071 "Find the general solution to the differential equation $y^{′} = y\\sin x$ for $y \\ne 0$." 13194139533402 17592186045585 false] [17592186069067 1071 "Find the general solution to the differential equation ${y}^{′}=y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ for $y\\ne 0.$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186069067 1074 "To find the general solution, we first separate the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\cancel{\\text{d}x}}\\,\\cancel{\\text{d}x} & =\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{y}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|y| & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation (in implicit form) is\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡|y|=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C.\n$$\n\nWe're required to write the solution in the form $y=y(x).$ By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|y|} & ={e}^{-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C} \\\\ |y| & ={e}^{-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C} \\\\ |y| & ={e}^{-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\cdot {e}^{C} \\\\ |y| & =K{e}^{-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K={e}^{C}$ and $K>0.$\n\nWe can drop the absolute value bars by allowing $K$ to be either positive or negative. Therefore, the most general solution is\n\n$$\ny=K{e}^{-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.\n$$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186069068 1068 #edb/ref 17592186065811 13194139533402 17592186045585 false] [17592186069068 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271869 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186069068 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186069068 1071 "Find the general solution to the differential equation $y^{′} = y^{2} + 1$." 13194139533402 17592186045585 false] [17592186069068 1071 "Find the general solution to the differential equation ${y}^{′}={y}^{2}+1.$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186069068 1074 "To find the general solution, we first separate the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={y}^{2}+1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={y}^{2}+1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{{y}^{2}+1}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{{y}^{2}+1}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{{y}^{2}+1}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\cancel{\\text{d}x}}\\,\\cancel{\\text{d}x} & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{{y}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{arctan}⁡(y) & =x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation (in implicit form) is\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(y)=x+C.\n$$\n\nWe're required to write the solution in the form $y=y(x).$ By doing this, we get\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x+C).\n$$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186083238 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272284 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186083238 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272285 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186083238 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272286 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186083309 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272246 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186083309 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272247 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186083309 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272248 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186083312 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272295 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186083313 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272292 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186083349 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272222 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186083349 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272223 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186083349 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272242 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186083349 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272347 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186083349 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272348 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186083541 1068 #edb/ref 17592186080111 13194139533402 17592186045585 false] [17592186083541 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271793 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186083541 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186083541 1071 "For the polynomial $f(x) = 10x^{6} - 52x^{2} + 50x - 8$, how many *potential* roots are given by the rational roots theorem?" 13194139533402 17592186045585 false] [17592186083541 1071 "For the polynomial $f(x)=10{x}^{6}-52{x}^{2}+50x-8,$ how many *potential* roots are given by the rational roots theorem?" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186083541 1074 "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial:\n\n- The constant term is $-8,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1,$ $\\pm 2,$ $\\pm 4,$ and $\\pm 8.$\n\n- The leading coefficient is $10,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1,$ $\\pm 2,$ $\\pm 5,$ and $\\pm 10.$\n\nFirst, we list out all the possibilities for the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm \\frac{1}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{5}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{10}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{2}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{5}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{10}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{4}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{4}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{4}{5}, & \\pm \\frac{4}{10}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{8}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{8}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{8}{5}, & \\pm \\frac{8}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we reduce the fractions to lowest terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{5}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{10}, \\\\ \\pm 2, & \\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{2}{5}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{5}, \\\\ \\pm 4, & \\pm 2, & \\pm \\frac{4}{5}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{5}, \\\\ \\pm 8, & \\pm 4, & \\pm \\frac{8}{5}, & \\pm \\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that several potential roots are duplicates (shown above in red). Removing these duplicates, and listing those remaining in order from least to greatest in absolute value, we have\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{1}{10},\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{5},\\,\\pm \\frac{2}{5},\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{2},\\,\\pm \\frac{4}{5},\\,\\pm 1,\\,\\pm \\frac{8}{5},\\,\\pm 2,\\,\\pm 4,\\,\\pm 8.\n$$\n\nThis makes a total of $20$ potential roots. (Remember that each $\\pm $ sign accounts for $2$ roots, and $2\\cdot 10=20.$)" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186083542 1068 #edb/ref 17592186080117 13194139533402 17592186045585 false] [17592186083542 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271543 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186083542 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186083542 1071 "For the polynomial $f(x) = 2x^{3} - 7x^{2} + 7x - 2$, how many *potential* roots are given by the rational roots theorem?" 13194139533402 17592186045585 false] [17592186083542 1071 "For the polynomial $f(x)=2{x}^{3}-7{x}^{2}+7x-2,$ how many *potential* roots are given by the rational roots theorem?" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186083542 1074 "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial:\n\n- The constant term is $-2,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1$ and $\\pm 2.$\n\n- The leading coefficient is $2,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1$ and $\\pm 2.$\n\nFirst, we list out all the possibilities for the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm \\frac{1}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{2}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we reduce the fractions to lowest terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, \\\\ \\pm 2, & \\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that some potential roots are duplicates (shown above in red). Removing these duplicates, and listing those remaining in order from least to greatest in absolute value, we have\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{1}{2},\\,\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 2.\n$$\n\nThis makes a total of $6$ potential roots. (Remember that each $\\pm $ sign accounts for $2$ roots, and $2\\cdot 3=6.$)" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186087759 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272088 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186087760 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272089 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186087825 1038 #edb/ref 17592186271970 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186087825 1038 #edb/ref 17592186271971 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186092242 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272200 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186092316 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272195 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186092316 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272197 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186092317 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272198 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186092317 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272199 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186092318 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272257 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186092319 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272345 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186092319 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272346 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186092333 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272253 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186092333 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272254 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186092333 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272255 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186092333 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272256 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186092559 1068 #edb/ref 17592186089495 13194139533402 17592186045585 false] [17592186092559 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271639 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186092559 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186092559 1071 "Which of the following expressions are polynomials?\n\n- $({x}^{2}+4x)-(-5{x}^{3}+3)$\n- $(2-{x}^{3})÷({x}^{2})$\n- $(2x-{x}^{3})\\cdot ({x}^{2}-x+1)$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186092559 1071 "Which of the following expressions are polynomials?\n\n1. $(x^{2} + 4x) - (-5x^{3} + 3)$\n2. $(2 - x^{3}) \\div (x^{2})$\n3. $(2x - x^{3}) \\cdot (x^{2} - x + 1)$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 false] [17592186092559 1074 "Recall that polynomials have the following closure properties:\n\n- Polynomials are closed under addition, subtraction, and multiplication. This means that the sum, difference, and product of two polynomials is *always* a polynomial.\n\n- Polynomials are *not* closed under division. This means that the quotient of two polynomials is *not* always a polynomial.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our expressions in turn.\n\n- Expression I is a polynomial. It's a difference between two polynomials, and polynomials are closed under subtraction.\n\n- Expression II is *not* a polynomial. Carrying out the division, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2-{x}^{3}}{{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}-x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is *not* a polynomial.\n\n- Expression III gives a polynomial. It's a product of two polynomials, and polynomials are closed under multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186096617 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272091 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186096617 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272092 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186096653 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272296 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186096653 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272392 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186096713 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272320 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186096713 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272323 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186096800 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272340 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186096800 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272391 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186097114 1068 #edb/ref 17592186094627 13194139533402 17592186045585 false] [17592186097114 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271879 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186097114 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186097114 1074 "First, we need to find the equation of the parabola. From the diagram, we see that the parabola has a vertex at $(-5,-6)$ and passes through the point $(-1,2).$\n\nThe general standard equation of an up- or down-opening parabola with vertex $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $(h,k)=(-5,-6)$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+5)^{2} & =4p(y+6).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find $p.$ Since the parabola passes through the point $(-1,2),$ we substitute $x=-1$ and $y=2$ into the equation and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1+5)^{2} & =4p(2+6) \\\\ 16 & =4p(8) \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+5)^{2} & =2(y+6).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen the parabola intersects the $x$-axis, we must have $y=0.$ So, we substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the parabola and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+5)^{2} & =2(0+6) \\\\ (x+5)^{2} & =12 \\\\ x+5 & =\\pm \\sqrt{12} \\\\ x & =\\pm 2\\sqrt{3}-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $x$-intercepts are $\\pm 2\\sqrt{3}-5.$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186097125 1068 #edb/ref 17592186094693 13194139533402 17592186045585 false] [17592186097125 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271742 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186097125 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186097125 1074 "First, we need to find the equation of the parabola. From the diagram, we see that the parabola has a vertex at $(5,-4)$ and passes through the point $(2,5).$\n\nThe general standard equation of an up- or down-opening parabola with vertex at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k),\n$$\n\nand substituting $(h,k)=(5,-4)$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n(x-5)^{2}=4p(y+4).\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find $p.$ Since the parabola passes through the point $(2,5),$ we substitute $x=2$ and $y=5$ into the equation and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2-5)^{2} & =4p(5+4) \\\\ 9 & =4p(9) \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-5)^{2} & =y+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen the parabola intersects the $y$-axis, we must have $x=0.$ So, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the parabola and solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0-5)^{2} & =y+4 \\\\ 25 & =y+4 \\\\ y & =25-4 \\\\ y & =21\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $y$-intercept is $21.$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186100894 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272202 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186100894 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272203 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186100894 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272204 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186100894 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272205 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186100894 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272206 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186100894 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272207 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186100894 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272208 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186100900 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272368 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186100957 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272217 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186100957 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272218 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186100957 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272219 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186100957 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272220 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186100957 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272221 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186100965 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272210 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186100965 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272211 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186100965 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272212 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186100965 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272213 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186100965 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272214 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186100965 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272215 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186100965 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272216 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186101161 1068 #edb/ref 17592186098271 13194139533402 17592186045585 false] [17592186101161 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271693 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186101161 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186101161 1071 "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{4y}{x},\\,x\\ne 0\n$$\n\n \n\nLet $f(x)={x}^{4}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}^{′}(x)=5{x}^{5}.$\n- Substituting $y=f(x)$ and ${y}^{′}={f}^{′}(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement.\n- The function $y=f(x)$ is a solution of the differential equation." 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186101161 1071 "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$y^{′} = \\frac{4y}{x}, x \\ne 0$\n\nLet $f(x) = x^{4}$. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $f^{′}(x) = 5x^{5}$.\n2. Substituting $y = f(x)$ and $y^{′} = f^{′}(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement.\n3. The function $y = f(x)$ is a solution of the differential equation." 13194139533402 17592186045585 false] [17592186101161 1074 "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Differentiating $f(x)={x}^{4},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =({x}^{4})^{′} \\\\  & =4{x}^{3} \\\\  & \\ne 5{x}^{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Substituting $y={x}^{4}$ and ${y}^{′}=4{x}^{3}$ into the original equation, we get a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{4y}{x} \\\\ (4{x}^{3}) & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{4({x}^{4})}{x} \\\\ 4{x}^{3} & =4{x}^{3}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, the function $y={x}^{4}$ is a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186101163 1068 #edb/ref 17592186098283 13194139533402 17592186045585 false] [17592186101163 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271697 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186101163 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186101163 1071 "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2y\n$$\n\n \n\nLet $f(x)={e}^{2x}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}^{′}(x)=2{e}^{2x}$\n- Substituting $y=f(x)$ and ${y}^{′}={f}^{′}(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement\n- The function $y=f(x)$ is a solution of the differential equation" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186101163 1071 "Consider the following differential equation.\n$y^{′} = 2y$\n\nLet $f(x) = e^{2x}$. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $f^{′}(x) = 2e^{2x}$\n2. Substituting $y = f(x)$ and $y^{′} = f^{′}(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement\n3. The function $y = f(x)$ is a solution of the differential equation" 13194139533402 17592186045585 false] [17592186101163 1074 "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Differentiating $f(x)={e}^{2x},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={({e}^{2x})}^{′} \\\\  & =2{e}^{2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Substituting $y={e}^{2x}$ and ${y}^{′}=2{e}^{2x}$ into the original equation, we get a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & \\overset{?}{=}2y \\\\ 2{e}^{2x} & =2{e}^{2x}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, the function $y={e}^{2x}$ is a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186101164 1068 #edb/ref 17592186098289 13194139533402 17592186045585 false] [17592186101164 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271622 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186101164 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186101164 1071 "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-2y.\n$$\n\n Which of the following is a solution to this equation?\n\n- $y=-2{e}^{-2x}$\n- $y=-2{e}^{x}$\n- $y=2{e}^{-x}+1$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186101164 1071 "Consider the differential equation\n$\\frac{dy}{dx} =-2y$.\n\nWhich of the following is a solution to this equation?\n\n1. $y =-2e^{-2x}$\n2. $y =-2e^{x}$\n3. $y = 2e^{-x} + 1$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 false] [17592186101164 1074 "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWe check each proposed solution by differentiating and substituting it into the original equation. \n\n- Differentiating $y=-2{e}^{-2x},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=4{e}^{-2x}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-2y \\\\ 4{e}^{-2x} & \\overset{?}{=}-2(-2{e}^{-2x}) \\\\ 4{e}^{-2x} & =4{e}^{-2x}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true*. Therefore, the function $y=-2{e}^{-2x}$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=-2{e}^{x},$ w