[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, by using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{2\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 0+0\\cdot 1}{{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we normalize each vector to get an orthonormal basis:\n\n- ${u}_{1}=\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- ${u}_{2}=\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{(-1)}^{2}}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6}\\end{aligned}].$\n\nTherefore, $B={{u}_{1},{u}_{2}}={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6}\\end{aligned}]}$ is an  orthonormal basis of $V.$   \n\nFinally, $a=\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6},$ $b=\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{3},$ $c=-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6}$ and   \n\n$$\n|a|+|b|+|c|=|\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6}|+|\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{3}\\,|+|-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6}|=\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{3}.\n$$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272136 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271948 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272136 1069 "q-140539" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272136 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272136 1071 "$$\nV=\\text{Span}{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]},\\,B={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]}\n$$\n\nConsider the subspace $V$ and an **orthonormal** basis $B$ of $V,$ given above. Given that $B$ is derived from $V$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $|a|+|b|+|c|?$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272136 1072 #uuid "2e6d1632-87e6-54dd-a8f9-23d096c3bcc7" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272136 1074 "First, let's denote\n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].$  \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$  \n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, by using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{0\\cdot 2+3\\cdot (-1)+(-3)\\cdot 1}{{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we normalize each vector to get an orthonormal basis:\n\n- ${u}_{1}=\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6}\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- ${u}_{2}=\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-2)^{2}}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\end{aligned}].$\n\nTherefore, $B={{u}_{1},{u}_{2}}={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\end{aligned}]}$ is an  orthonormal basis of $V.$   \n\nFinally, $a=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3},$ $b=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3},$ $c=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}$ and  \n\n$$\n|a|+|b|+|c|=|\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}|+|\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}|+|-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}|=\\sqrt{3}.\n$$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272137 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271892 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272137 1069 "q-140549" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272137 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272137 1071 "Which curve is defined by the equation $3{x}_{1}^{2}-6{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-5{x}_{2}^{2}=24?$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272137 1072 #uuid "fe9938a7-e3f2-57da-8c02-e91f20cff686" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272137 1074 "Consider the quadratic form $Q(x)=3{x}_{1}^{2}-6{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-5{x}_{2}^{2}.$ To determine the curve defined by the given equation, we reduce $Q(x)$ to its canonical (diagonal) form.\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of $Q(x),$ which is\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -3 \\\\ -3 & -5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNext, we find the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}3-\\lambda  & -3 \\\\ -3 & -5-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda -24 & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =4,\\,-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, our quadratic form can be reduced to $4{y}_{1}^{2}-6{y}_{2}^{2}.$ \n\nIn the new variables, the curve has the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{y}_{1}^{2}-6{y}_{2}^{2} & =24 \\\\ \\frac{{y}_{1}^{2}}{6}-\\frac{{y}_{2}^{2}}{4} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{y}_{1}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{6})^{2}}-\\frac{{y}_{2}^{2}}{{2}^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a hyperbola with semiaxes $\\sqrt{6}$ and $2.$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272138 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271716 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272138 1069 "q-140556" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272138 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272138 1071 "Consider the following set of functions:\n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(t)=2(t-1),\\,{f}_{2}(t)={e}^{t},\\,{f}_{3}(t)={e}^{(t-1)^{2}}\n$$\n\nGiven that the inner product in the vector space $C[0,2]$ of continuous functions over $[0,2]$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{2}f(t)g(t)\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n- ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n- $S={{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{3}(t)}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272138 1072 #uuid "1e4b04d4-e7d3-5042-809a-2a3512a7a902" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272138 1074 "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is called orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩ & =⟨2(t-1),{e}^{t}⟩ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}2(t-1)({e}^{t})\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's use integration by parts. We denote\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=2(t-1) & ⟹ & {u}^{′}=2, \\\\ {v}^{′}={e}^{t} & ⟹ & v=\\int {v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}t=\\int {e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t={e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, applying the integration by parts formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}2(t-1){e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t & =uv{|}_{0}^{2}-{\\int }_{0}^{2}{u}^{′}v\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(2(t-1){e}^{t}){|}_{0}^{2}-{\\int }_{0}^{2}2{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(2{e}^{2}-(-2))-2{e}^{t}{|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =2{e}^{2}+2-(2{e}^{2}-2) \\\\  & =4\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥̸{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement II is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{3}(t)⟩ & =⟨2(t-1),{e}^{(t-1)^{2}}⟩ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}2(t-1){e}^{(t-1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={e}^{(t-1)^{2}}{|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =e-e \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${f}_{1}(t)⊥̸{f}_{3}(t),$ the set $S$ is *not* orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272139 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271751 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272139 1069 "q-140563" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272139 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272139 1071 "Solve for $x$ in $Ax=b$ given that the matrix $A$ is orthogonal, and \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} & \\frac{2}{3} & -\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{15} & \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3} & \\frac{4\\sqrt{5}}{15}\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ \\sqrt{5} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272139 1072 #uuid "37be5226-f355-5da6-9310-01c6af117505" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272139 1074 "The inverse of an orthogonal matrix is equal to its transpose.\n\nHence, since $A$ is orthogonal, we have ${A}^{-1}={A}^{T}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax & =b \\\\ {A}^{-1}Ax & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\ x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\ x & ={A}^{T}b \\\\ x & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{15} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} & 0 & \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{4\\sqrt{5}}{15}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ \\sqrt{5} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272140 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271916 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272140 1069 "q-140565" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272140 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272140 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the $n\\times n$ orthogonal matrix $Q$ and the $n\\times n$ matrix $P?$\n\n- If $\\lambda $ is a real eigenvalue of $Q,$ then $\\lambda =\\pm 1$\n- If the determinant of $P$ is either $1$ or $-1,$ then $P$ is orthogonal\n- $Q-I$ is singular" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272140 1072 #uuid "a3d61daa-c943-5ecf-a98c-7f9bcd210491" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272140 1074 "Recall that a matrix $Q$ is orthogonal if and only if\n\n$$\nQ{Q}^{T}={Q}^{T}Q=I.\n$$\n\nTo check that a matrix is orthogonal, it is sufficient to check that the above equation is satisfied.\n\nMoreover, we have the following properties:\n\n- For any orthogonal matrix $Q,$ we have ${Q}^{-1}={Q}^{T}.$\n\n- $(AB)^{T}={B}^{T}{A}^{T}$ for conformable matrices $A$ and $B.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. If $\\lambda $ is an eigenvalue of an orthogonal matrix $Q$ then $\\lambda =\\pm 1$\n\n- Statement II is false. Every orthogonal matrix has the determinant equal to $\\pm 1$, but not every matrix with the determinant equal to $\\pm 1$ is orthogonal. As a counterexample, consider the following matrix:\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe determinant of $P$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(P)=2\\cdot 1-1\\cdot 1=1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n however, $P$ is not orthogonal since, for example, the first column is not normalized.\n\n- Statement III is false. As a counterexample, consider the following orthogonal matrix:\n\n$$\nQ=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{5} & -\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWe have that\n\n$$\nQ-I=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{5} & -\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{5} & -\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5} & -\\frac{2}{5}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nand $Q-I$ is not singular since, for example, it doesn't have proportional rows or columns.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272141 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271574 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272141 1069 "q-140573" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272141 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272141 1071 "![](images/27/27bb4ee622f0b80e4b1a6edfae2c42f0676cafd0f9ad6150b979b32dbdabfc62.png)\n\nWhich integral expression gives the total area enclosed between the curve $y=4{x}^{3}-{x}^{5}$ and the $x$-axis, as shown above?" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272141 1072 #uuid "6383ab1d-97dd-5a59-bcc5-a3b6461bc8bf" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272141 1074 "First, we find the limits of integration. We find the $x$-intercepts of the curve as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{3}-{x}^{5} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3}(4-{x}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3}(2-x)(2+x) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have $x=\\pm 2$ or $x=0.$ Since $0\\in [-2,2],$ we have two regions: \n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=-2$ to $x=0.$\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=0$ to $x=2.$\n\nNotice that for $x\\in [-2,0]$ the region lies completely below the $x$-axis. Hence, the value of\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-2}^{0}(4{x}^{3}-{x}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwill be negative. Therefore, the area that we want is given by\n\n$$\n{A}_{1}=-{\\int }_{-2}^{0}(4{x}^{3}-{x}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-2}^{0}-(4{x}^{3}-{x}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nFor $x\\in [0,2]$, we have\n\n$$\n{A}_{2}={\\int }_{0}^{2}(4{x}^{3}-{x}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nFinally, the total area is\n\n$$\nA={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}={\\int }_{-2}^{0}-(4{x}^{3}-{x}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{2}(4{x}^{3}-{x}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272142 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271610 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272142 1069 "q-140574" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272142 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272142 1071 "![](images/41/41e7b3f6588f7f4dd55096fb95bbe517a053c11f80c43aab171d80829864d2c3.png)\n\nThe total area $A$ of the region between the curve $y=3x-2{x}^{2}$ and the $x$-axis from $x=1$ to $x=\\sqrt{10}$ is given by the integral expression below. What is the value of $a?$\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{1}^{a}(3x-2{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x-{\\int }_{a}^{\\sqrt{10}}(3x-2{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272142 1072 #uuid "b49a64db-4fbd-5d7e-8cd7-52e60a104d30" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272142 1074 "To determine the missing limit of integration, we need to find the $x$-intercept of the curve $y=3x-2{x}^{2}.$ So, we set $y=0$ and solve for $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x-2{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ x(3-2x) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\,\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{3}{2}\\in [1,4],$ we have two regions:\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=1$ to $x=\\frac{3}{2}.$\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=\\frac{3}{2}$ to $x=\\sqrt{10}.$\n\nSo, $a=\\frac{3}{2}$ and the final expression will be\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{1}^{3/2}(3x-2{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x-{\\int }_{3/2}^{\\sqrt{10}}(3x-2{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272143 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271688 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272143 1069 "q-140577" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272143 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272143 1071 "![](images/a4/a4871287696f20009bd7d2b8793965c9f68e35faa4ad150477643f1d3ec77e38.png)\n\nWhich integral expression gives the total area enclosed between the curve $y={x}^{5}-{x}^{4}-6{x}^{3}$ and the $x$-axis, as shown above?" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272143 1072 #uuid "2b201971-ef41-565e-a1de-5bc3aa9d08d3" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272143 1074 "First, we find the limits of integration. We find the $x$-intercepts of the curve as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{5}-{x}^{4}-6{x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3}({x}^{2}-x-6) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3}(x+2)(x-3) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have $x=-2,x=0$ or $x=3.$ Since $0\\in [-2,3],$ we have two regions: \n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=-2$ to $x=0.$\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=0$ to $x=3.$\n\nFor $x\\in [-2,0]$, we have\n\n$$\n{A}_{1}={\\int }_{-2}^{0}({x}^{5}-{x}^{4}-6{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNotice that for $x\\in [0,3]$ the region lies completely below the $x$-axis. Hence, the value of\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{0}^{3}({x}^{5}-{x}^{4}-6{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwill be negative. Therefore, the area that we want is given by\n\n$$\n{A}_{2}=-{\\int }_{0}^{3}({x}^{5}-{x}^{4}-6{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{0}^{3}-({x}^{5}-{x}^{4}-6{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nFinally, the total area is\n\n$$\nA={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}={\\int }_{-2}^{0}({x}^{5}-{x}^{4}-6{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{3}-({x}^{5}-{x}^{4}-6{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272144 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271638 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272144 1069 "q-140580" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272144 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272144 1071 "![](images/2a/2a32d95d98a2c2cefe08b492c368a99df61a8cc3a1f66018a6dfe444e65146b8.png)\n\nWhich integral expression gives the total area enclosed between the curve $y=9{x}^{3}-{x}^{5}$ and the $x$-axis, as shown above?" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272144 1072 #uuid "da59a618-882d-5350-b181-63ab13e7d365" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272144 1074 "First, we find the limits of integration. We find the $x$-intercepts of the curve as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{x}^{3}-{x}^{5} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3}(9-{x}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3}(3-x)(3+x) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have $x=\\pm 3$ or $x=0.$ Since $0\\in [-3,3],$ we have two regions: \n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=-3$ to $x=0.$\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=0$ to $x=3.$\n\nNotice that for $x\\in [-3,0]$ the region lies completely below the $x$-axis. Hence, the value of\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-3}^{0}(9{x}^{3}-{x}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwill be negative. Therefore, the area that we want is given by\n\n$$\n{A}_{2}=-{\\int }_{-3}^{0}(9{x}^{3}-{x}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-3}^{0}-(9{x}^{3}-{x}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nFor $x\\in [0,3]$, we have\n\n$$\n{A}_{2}={\\int }_{0}^{3}(9{x}^{3}-{x}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nFinally, the total area is\n\n$$\nA={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}={\\int }_{-3}^{0}-(9{x}^{3}-{x}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{3}(9{x}^{3}-{x}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272145 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271567 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272145 1069 "q-140581" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272145 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272145 1071 "![](images/72/72ecb00845c906fd44da9b5f559fb06445b3d8ac154064743fd433cc895e1916.png)\n\nThe total area $A$ of the region between the curve $y=\\frac{4}{x-2}+1$ and the $x$-axis from $x=-5$ to $x=0$ is given by the integral expression below. What is the value of $a?$\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-5}^{a}(\\frac{4}{x-2}+1)\\text{d}x-{\\int }_{a}^{0}(\\frac{4}{x-2}+1)\\text{d}x.\n$$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272145 1072 #uuid "cb6e0f27-d15a-5ad0-98ed-e405a466ea03" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272145 1074 "To determine the missing limit of integration, we need to find the $x$-intercept of the curve $y=\\frac{4}{x-2}+1.$ So, we set $y=0$ and solve for $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{x-2}+1 & =0 \\\\ \\frac{4}{x-2} & =-1 \\\\ 4 & =-(x-2) \\\\ 4 & =-x+2 \\\\ 2 & =-x \\\\ x & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $-2\\in [-5,0],$ we have two regions:\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=-5$ to $x=-2.$\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=-2$ to $x=0.$\n\nSo, $a=-2$ and the final expression will be\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-5}^{-2}(\\frac{4}{x-2}+1)\\text{d}x-{\\int }_{-2}^{0}(\\frac{4}{x-2}+1)\\text{d}x.\n$$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272146 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271663 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272146 1069 "q-140584" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272146 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272146 1071 "![](images/7d/7d49ecfb67ace2bf8fa93b44c24796bbcc9953b3ee9976d30c9e50e28f01ff04.png)\n\nThe total area $A$ of the region between the curve $y=\\frac{1}{16}(x+2)^{4}-1$ and the $x$-axis from $x=-5$ to $x=-1$ is given by the integral expression below. What is the value of $a?$\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-5}^{a}(\\frac{1}{16}(x+2)^{4}-1)\\text{d}x-{\\int }_{a}^{-1}(\\frac{1}{16}(x+2)^{4}-1)\\text{d}x.\n$$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272146 1072 #uuid "2fdb8b6b-e1ab-52c9-8e66-747c1914e630" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272146 1074 "To determine the missing limit of integration, we need to find the $x$-intercept of the curve $y=\\frac{1}{16}(x+2)^{4}-1.$ So, we set $y=0$ and solve for $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{16}(x+2)^{4}-1 & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{16}(x+2)^{4} & =1 \\\\ (x+2)^{4} & =16 \\\\ \\sqrt[4]{(x+2)^{4}} & =\\pm \\sqrt[4]{16} \\\\ x+2 & =\\pm \\sqrt[4]{{2}^{4}} \\\\ x+2 & =\\pm 2 \\\\ x & =-4,\\,0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe only solution between $x=-5$ and $x=-1$ is $x=-4.$\n\nSince $-4\\in [-5,-1],$ we have two regions:\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=-5$ to $x=-4.$\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=-4$ to $x=-1.$\n\nSo, $a=-4$ and the final expression will be\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-5}^{-4}(\\frac{1}{16}(x+2)^{4}-1)\\text{d}x-{\\int }_{-4}^{-1}(\\frac{1}{16}(x+2)^{4}-1)\\text{d}x.\n$$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272147 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271872 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272147 1069 "q-1406" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272147 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272147 1071 "Let $u=3i-2k$ and $v=-2i+j+5k.$ Determine $u\\times v.$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272147 1072 #uuid "55f94724-9237-566f-b3f0-87f89c5fdc08" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272147 1074 "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\times v & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 3 & 0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 5\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =(0\\cdot 5-(-2)\\cdot 1)i-(3\\cdot 5-(-2)\\cdot (-2))j+(3\\cdot 1-0\\cdot (-2))k \\\\  & =(0+2)i-(15-4)j+(3-0)k \\\\  & =2i-11j+3k.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272148 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271765 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272148 1069 "q-140602" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272148 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272148 1071 "Given the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ -6 & -4\\end{aligned}]$\nand $\\,f(x)={x}^{16}-4{x}^{14}+7x-3\\,$, find $f(A).$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272148 1072 #uuid "af2db92b-ede0-52d7-a7d4-1043a63d9db0" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272148 1074 "Let's start by finding the characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda ):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(\\lambda ) & =\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I) \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}4-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ -6 & -4-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(4-\\lambda )(-4-\\lambda )+12 \\\\  & ={\\lambda }^{2}-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's divide $f(x)$ by $p(x)$ using long division:\n\n|  | $\\,\\,{x}^{14}$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}^{2}-4\\,$ | $\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{{x}^{16}\\,-\\,4{x}^{14}\\,+\\,7x\\,-\\,3}$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n|  | ${x}^{16}$ | $-$ | $4{x}^{14}$ |  |  |  |  |\n|  |  |  |  |  | $7x$ | $-$ | $3$ |\n\nTherefore, we can write $f(x)$ as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =q(x)p(x)+r(x) \\\\  & =q(x)p(x)+(7x-3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $q(x)={x}^{14}.$ Replacing $x$ with $A,$ we have\n\n$$\nf(A)=q(A)p(A)+(7A-3I).\n$$\n\nAccording to the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we have $p(A)=O.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(A) & =q(A)p(A)+(7A-3I) \\\\  & =q(A)\\cdot O+(7A-3I) \\\\  & =7A-3I \\\\  & =7[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ -6 & -4\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}28 & 14 \\\\ -42 & -28\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}25 & 14 \\\\ -42 & -31\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272149 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271705 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272149 1069 "q-140603" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272149 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272149 1071 "![](images/2f/2ffa9585853d1230c9664a27dffcd832aec5cdff3fa1d3f01c9012d2835d101e.png)\n\nFind the total area of the region between the curve $y={x}^{2}-2x$ and the $x$-axis from $x=0$ to $x=3,$ as shown above." 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272149 1072 #uuid "a3bbaade-b875-5624-a7f9-38beb32b501f" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272149 1074 "First, we find the intersections of the curve with the $x$-axis:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-2x & =0 \\\\ x(x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $2\\in [0,3],$ we have two regions: \n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=0$ to $x=2.$\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=2$ to $x=3.$\n\nNotice that the region for $x\\in [0,2]$ lies completely below the $x$-axis. Hence, the value of\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{0}^{2}({x}^{2}-2x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwill be negative. Therefore, the area that we want is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =-{\\int }_{0}^{2}({x}^{2}-2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{2}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x+2{\\int }_{0}^{2}x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}\\,{|}_{0}^{2}+{x}^{2}\\,{|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}[{2}^{3}-{0}^{3}]+[{2}^{2}-{0}^{2}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}[8-0]+4 \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{3}+4 \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $x\\in [2,3],$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & ={\\int }_{2}^{3}({x}^{2}-2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{3}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x-2{\\int }_{2}^{3}x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}\\,{|}_{2}^{3}-{x}^{2}\\,{|}_{2}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[{3}^{3}-{2}^{3}]-[{3}^{2}-{2}^{2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[27-8]-[9-4] \\\\  & =\\frac{19}{3}-5 \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the total area is\n\n$$\nA={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}=\\frac{4}{3}+\\frac{4}{3}=\\frac{8}{3}.\n$$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272150 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271525 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272150 1069 "q-140606" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272150 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272150 1071 "If $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ with characteristic polynomial $p(x)={x}^{2}+3x-2,$ then ${A}^{4}=$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272150 1072 #uuid "dc87eb6a-695f-5bbf-80b7-7a8552f10296" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272150 1074 "First, let's divide the polynomial $f(x)={x}^{4}$ by characteristic polynomial $p(x)={x}^{2}+3x-2,$ using long division:\n\n|  | $\\,\\,{x}^{2}$ | $-$ | $3x$ | $+$ | $11$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}^{2}+3x-2\\,$ | $\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{{x}^{4}\\,\\,\\,}$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n|  | ${x}^{4}$ | $+$ | $3{x}^{3}$ | $-$ | $2{x}^{2}$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n|  |  | $-$ | $3{x}^{3}$ | $+$ | $2{x}^{2}$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n|  |  | $-$ | $3{x}^{3}$ | $-$ | $9{x}^{2}$ | $+$ | $6x$ |  |  |  |  |\n|  |  |  |  |  | $11{x}^{2}$ | $-$ | $6x$ |  |  |  |  |\n|  |  |  |  |  | $11{x}^{2}$ | $+$ | $33x$ | $-$ | $22$ |  |  |\n|  |  |  |  |  |  | $-$ | $39x$ | $+$ | $22$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we can write ${x}^{4}$ as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{4} & =q(x)p(x)+(-39x+22),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $q(x)={x}^{2}-3x+11.$  Replacing $x$ with $A,$ we have\n\n$$\n{A}^{4}=q(A)p(A)+(-39A+22I).\n$$\n\nNow, according to the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we have $p(A)=O.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{4} & =q(A)p(A)+(-39A+22I) \\\\  & =q(A)\\cdot O+(-39A+22I) \\\\  & =-39A+22I.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272151 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271945 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272151 1069 "q-140607" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272151 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272151 1071 "Find the total area of the region between the curve $y=3x-{x}^{2}$ and the $x$-axis from $x=1$ to $x=4.$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272151 1072 #uuid "04b86384-ff85-50f1-b5ea-5fd29d41b106" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272151 1074 "First, we find the intersections of the curve with the $x$-axis:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x-{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ x(3-x) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\,3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $3\\in [1,4],$ we have two regions: \n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=1$ to $x=3.$\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=3$ to $x=4.$\n\n![](images/21/219d7aa5b976c97d47a96d0c94c3d61d139d5ddddc55b82d0df68f19ad6d5149.png)\n\nNotice that the region for $x\\in [3,4]$ lies completely below the $x$-axis. Hence, the value of\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{3}^{4}(3x-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwill be negative. Therefore, the area that we want is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}(3x-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{1}^{3}x\\,\\text{d}x-{\\int }_{1}^{3}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}\\,{|}_{1}^{3}-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}\\,{|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}[{3}^{2}-{1}^{2}]-\\frac{1}{3}[{3}^{3}-{1}^{3}] \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}[9-1]-\\frac{1}{3}[27-1] \\\\  & =12-\\frac{26}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $x\\in [3,4],$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =-{\\int }_{3}^{4}(3x-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-3{\\int }_{3}^{4}x\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{3}^{4}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}\\,{|}_{3}^{4}+\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}\\,{|}_{3}^{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}[{4}^{2}-{3}^{2}]+\\frac{1}{3}[{4}^{3}-{3}^{3}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}[16-9]+\\frac{1}{3}[64-27] \\\\  & =-\\frac{21}{2}+\\frac{37}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the total area is\n\n$$\nA={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}=\\frac{10}{3}+\\frac{11}{6}=\\frac{31}{6}.\n$$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272152 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271930 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272152 1069 "q-1407" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272152 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272152 1071 "Let $a=⟨0,1,6⟩$ and $b=⟨13,0,0⟩.$ Find $a\\times b.$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272152 1072 #uuid "4da79104-318a-574d-9235-024232815019" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272152 1074 "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\times b & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 1 & 6 \\\\ 13 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}0 & 6 \\\\ 13 & 0\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 13 & 0\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =(1\\cdot 0-6\\cdot 0)i-(0\\cdot 0-6\\cdot 13)j+(0\\cdot 0-1\\cdot 13)k \\\\  & =(0-0)i-(0-78)j+(0-13)k \\\\  & =78j-13k.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272153 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271754 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272153 1069 "q-140717" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272153 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272153 1071 "Find an eigenvector of $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -3 & -3\\end{aligned}]$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2.$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272153 1072 #uuid "00737dc0-f397-573a-80e1-d038ce87150d" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272153 1074 "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =3.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -3 & -3\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -3 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -3 & -5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}-5{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -3 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -3 & -5 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=(-1){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -3 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -3 & -5 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-3{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-3{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-(\\frac{1}{4}){R}_{2} \\\\ {R}_{2}:=(\\frac{1}{4}){R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $3$rd). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{3}=0.$ Substituting this in the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{2}+2{x}_{3} \\\\  & ={x}_{2}+2(0) \\\\  & ={x}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector\n$v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272154 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271921 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272154 1069 "q-140719" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272154 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272154 1071 "Find an eigenvector of $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-3.$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272154 1072 #uuid "f235e8a4-87f8-5bc9-a7bf-ca7b62641c4e" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272154 1074 "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =-3.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]-(-3)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 7\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}5{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ {x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 7{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 7 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-7){R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $3$rd). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{3}=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{-{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{-{x}_{2}+2(0)}{5} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{x}_{2}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{{x}_{2}}{5} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=5,$ we get the eigenvector\n$v=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272155 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271875 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272155 1069 "q-140720" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272155 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272155 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are eigenvectors that correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=2$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3$ of the matrix $A,$ given above. Find the third eigenvector ${v}_{3}=[a,\\,b,\\,c]^{T}$ such that the set ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is linearly independent. What is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}?$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272155 1072 #uuid "f3c0726d-3efe-576b-8418-4bde00e5fffc" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272155 1074 "Since $A$ is an upper triangular matrix, we have that its eigenvalues are the entries in the main diagonal, that is, ${\\lambda }_{1}=2,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=1.$ \n\nAlso, since we obtained three distinct eigenvalues, the set of eigenvectors ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}},$ where each vector corresponds to different eigenvalue, will be linearly independent.\n\nNow, recall that ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=2$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3.$ Thus, the third eigenvector ${v}_{3}$ must correspond to ${\\lambda }_{3}=1.$ \n\nTo compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =1.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]-1[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -4{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 0=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the augmented matrix $M$ is already in its row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{2}=\\frac{1}{4}{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =-{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}{x}_{3}+2{x}_{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{4}{x}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7}{4}{x}_{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{3}=4,$ we get the following eigenvector:\n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{7}{1}=7.$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272156 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271670 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272156 1069 "q-140747" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272156 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272156 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ shown above. Which of the following is a basis of the eigenspace corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =5?$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272156 1072 #uuid "6d07b0e1-0dbd-518a-9e47-6270ba41b78a" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272156 1074 "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =5.$ So, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 5\\end{aligned}]-5[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-3{x}_{1}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}↔{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 