dot (\\overset{―}{-3})+(1+2i)\\cdot \\overset{―}{1}+1\\cdot \\overset{―}{2} \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot (2-i)+2\\cdot (-3)+(1+2i)\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =(-2+i)+(-6)+(1+2\\text{i})+2 \\\\  & =-5+3i.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272197 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271947 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272197 1069 "q-141040" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272197 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272197 1071 "![](images/dc/dcb8b5dee06f59322f2f365512065857c79715be39f96a50d91cc9062991aa6e.png)\n\nFind $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for the polar curve $r=10{e}^{-\\theta }$ (shown above) given that\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(\\theta )=10{e}^{-\\theta }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ),\\,{x}^{′}(\\theta )=-10{e}^{-\\theta }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ).\n$$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272197 1072 #uuid "3489c0dc-af49-5e9c-8486-3990dc32dfdd" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272197 1074 "To find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ we use the rule\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{10{e}^{-\\theta }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}{-10{e}^{-\\theta }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272198 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271577 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272198 1069 "q-141046" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272198 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272198 1071 "Find the slope of the tangent line to the polar curve $r={e}^{2\\theta }$ at $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ given that\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(\\theta )={e}^{2\\theta }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ),\\,{x}^{′}(\\theta )={e}^{2\\theta }(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\n$$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272198 1072 #uuid "d9b01c22-41c9-560d-9b14-7e8abb48dc24" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272198 1074 "To find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ we use the rule\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}.\n$$\n\nWe have,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & ={e}^{\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{4}+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & ={e}^{\\pi /2}(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,{e}^{\\pi /2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & ={e}^{\\pi /2}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{4}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & ={e}^{\\pi /2}(2\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,{e}^{\\pi /2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nHence, the slope of the tangent line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{\\theta =\\pi /4} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})}{{x}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,{e}^{\\pi /2})}{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,{e}^{\\pi /2})} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272199 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271740 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272199 1069 "q-141048" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272199 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272199 1071 "Find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for the polar curve $r=2\\theta $  at $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ given that\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(\\theta )=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,{x}^{′}(\\theta )=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$" 1319