eta }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y={e}^{-3\\theta }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTo compute our derivative, we first need to calculate ${x}^{′}(\\theta )$ and ${y}^{′}(\\theta ):$ \n\n- First, we compute the derivative of $x$ with respect to $\\theta $ using the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\theta ) & ={({e}^{-3\\theta }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{′} \\\\  & ={({e}^{-3\\theta })}^{′}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +{e}^{-3\\theta }\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{′} \\\\  & =-3{e}^{-3\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +{e}^{-3\\theta }\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =-3{e}^{-3\\theta }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -{e}^{-3\\theta }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =-{e}^{-3\\theta }(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute the derivative of $y$ with respect to $\\theta $ using the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\theta ) & ={({e}^{-3\\theta }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{′} \\\\  & ={({e}^{-3\\theta })}^{′}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +{e}^{-3\\theta }\\cdot (\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{′} \\\\  & =-3{e}^{-3\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +{e}^{-3\\theta }\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =-3{e}^{-3\\theta }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +{e}^{-3\\theta }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={e}^{-3\\theta }(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-3\\theta }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}{-{e}^{-3\\theta }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272201 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271748 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272201 1069 "q-141051" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272201 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272201 1071 "![](images/6c/6c07657992f5c4f708cd7546902c5e26206bfa41f291a68ec7b582cb18a167fe.png)\n\nConsider the polar curve $r=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2,$ shown above. Given that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta +a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta +b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\n$$\n\nfind the value of $a-b.$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272201 1072 #uuid "8cfb92f2-65f8-5b47-be13-0657b1949d40" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272201 1074 "To find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use the formula for differentiating parametric curves:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}\n$$\n\nFirst, we recall that to convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $r=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta +2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =3{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we used the identity $\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$ \n\nTo compute our derivative, we first need to calculate ${x}^{′}(\\theta )$ and ${y}^{′}(\\theta ):$ \n\n- First, we compute the derivative of $x$ with respect to $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\theta ) & ={(\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta +2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{′} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta )}^{′}+(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\cdot 2)+2(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute the derivative of $y$ with respect to $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\theta ) & ={(3{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{′} \\\\  & ={(3{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}^{′}+{(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{′} \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )+(-2)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=2,b=-2,$ and so \n\n$$\na-b=2-(-2)=4.\n$$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272202 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271565 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272202 1069 "q-141080" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272202 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272202 1071 "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2y\n$$\n\n \n\nLet $f(x)=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}^{′}(x)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x$\n- Substituting $y=f(x)$ and ${y}^{′}={f}^{′}(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement\n- The function $y=f(x)$ is a solution of the differential equation" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272202 1072 #uuid "5c28aa3d-73cf-552e-9e05-dfc5bcb67151" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272202 1074 "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Differentiating $f(x)=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)}^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x \\\\  & \\ne 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Substituting $y=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x$ and ${y}^{′}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x$ into the original equation, we get a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & \\overset{?}{=}2y \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x & \\overset{?}{=}2(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x & \\ne \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, the function $y=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x$ is not a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None.\"" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272203 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271646 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272203 1069 "q-141081" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272203 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272203 1071 "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\n$$\n\n \n\nLet $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}^{′}(x)=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x$\n- Substituting $y=f(x)$ and ${y}^{′}={f}^{′}(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement\n- The function $y=f(x)$ is a solution of the differential equation" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272203 1072 #uuid "82065c87-2fb6-51b8-8d32-9c57d0719fb4" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272203 1074 "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Differentiating $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)}^{′} \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Substituting $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x$ and ${y}^{′}=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x$ into the original equation, we get a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & \\overset{?}{=}y\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x \\\\ -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x \\\\ -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x & \\ne {cos}^{2}⁡2x\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, the function $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x$ is not a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272204 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271530 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272204 1069 "q-141082" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272204 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272204 1071 "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{{e}^{y}}\n$$\n\n \n\nLet $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{x}$\n- Substituting $y=f(x)$ and ${y}^{′}={f}^{′}(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement\n- The function $y=f(x)$ is a solution of the differential equation" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272204 1072 #uuid "9b7a88f7-a074-5f69-9224-f1585f849cc4" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272204 1074 "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Differentiating $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Substituting $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ and ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{x}$ into the original equation, we get a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{1}{{e}^{y}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{1}{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{x} & =\\frac{1}{x}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, the function $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272205 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271673 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272205 1069 "q-141084" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272205 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272205 1071 "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}},\\,x\\ne 0\n$$\n\n \n\nLet $f(x)=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}^{′}(x)=-\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}$\n- Substituting $y=f(x)$ and ${y}^{′}={f}^{′}(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement\n- The function $y=f(x)$ is a solution of the differential equation" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272205 1072 #uuid "c4402732-29b4-541b-a6e6-a372fd33bf97" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272205 1074 "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Differentiating $f(x)=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}} \\\\  & \\ne -\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Substituting $y=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}$ and ${y}^{′}=\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}$ into the original equation, we get a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{{x}^{3}} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{{x}^{3}} & \\ne -\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, the function $y=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}$ is not a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None.\"" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272206 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271803 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272206 1069 "q-141086" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272206 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272206 1071 "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-3y\n$$\n\n \n\nLet $f(x)=3{e}^{-x}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}^{′}(x)=3{e}^{-x}$\n- Substituting $y=f(x)$ and ${y}^{′}={f}^{′}(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement\n- The function $y=f(x)$ is a solution of the differential equation" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272206 1072 #uuid "33a5a8c7-40f0-5353-a590-88087e77753a" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272206 1074 "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Differentiating $f(x)=3{e}^{-x},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={(3{e}^{-x})}^{′} \\\\  & =-3{e}^{-x} \\\\  & \\ne 3{e}^{-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Substituting $y=3{e}^{-x}$ and ${y}^{′}=-3{e}^{-x}$ into the original equation, we get a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & \\overset{?}{=}-3y \\\\ -3{e}^{-x} & \\overset{?}{=}-3(3{e}^{-x}) \\\\ -3{e}^{-x} & \\ne -9{e}^{-x}\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, the function $y=3{e}^{-x}$ is not a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None.\"" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272207 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271912 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272207 1069 "q-141088" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272207 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272207 1071 "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{4x}{{e}^{y/2}}\n$$\n\n \n\nLet $f(x)=4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{4x}$\n- Substituting $y=f(x)$ and ${y}^{′}={f}^{′}(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement\n- The function $y=f(x)$ is a solution of the differential equation" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272207 1072 #uuid "9c8904e1-5f17-5951-8dbc-61b76cd0babc" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272207 1074 "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Differentiating $f(x)=4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{x} \\\\  & \\ne \\frac{1}{4x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Substituting $y=4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ and ${y}^{′}=\\frac{4}{x}$ into the original equation, we get a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{4x}{{e}^{y/2}} \\\\ \\frac{4}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{4x}{{e}^{(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)/2}} \\\\ \\frac{4}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{4x}{{e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}} \\\\ \\frac{4}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{4x}{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}^{2}}} \\\\ \\frac{4}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{4x}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{4}{x} & =\\frac{4}{x}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, the function $y=4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272208 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271917 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272208 1069 "q-141089" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272208 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272208 1071 "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{2y}{x},\\,x\\ne 0\n$$\n\n \n\nLet $f(x)=-3{x}^{2}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}^{′}(x)=-5x$\n- Substituting $y=f(x)$ and ${y}^{′}={f}^{′}(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement\n- The function $y=f(x)$ is a solution of the differential equation" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272208 1072 #uuid "8806c061-892c-5fd2-baf1-cd903aa5ea31" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272208 1074 "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Differentiating $f(x)=-3{x}^{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={(-3{x}^{2})}^{′} \\\\  & =-3\\cdot 2x \\\\  & =-6x \\\\  & \\ne -5x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Substituting $y=-3{x}^{2}$ and ${y}^{′}=-6x$ into the original equation, we get a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2y}{x} \\\\ -6x & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2(-3{x}^{2})}{x} \\\\ -6x & =-6x\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, the function $y=-3{x}^{2}$ is a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272209 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271747 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272209 1069 "q-141090" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272209 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272209 1071 "![](images/f7/f74ae80071413963d1b71f91b99ae625edf6e285bb068be7b969483672f1288e.png)\n\nGiven the graph above, solve the inequality $-(x+2)(x-4)\\le 0.$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272209 1072 #uuid "7d764690-b137-5254-82de-0bc2368d66ae" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272209 1074 "Notice that our inequality\n\n$$\n-(x+2)(x-4)\\le 0,\n$$\n\nis a \"*less than or equal to*\" inequality. Therefore, we need to find the values of $x$ for which the parabola \n\n$$\ny=-(x+2)(x-4)\n$$\n\nis *below* the $x$-axis or *touches* this axis. We mark these values of $x$ in blue in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/12/12348273c26017bb59327a79b85a5b0fb3c0fc2f37583f7dd9ec30cfcb20b019.png)\n\nSo, the solution to the inequality is $x\\le -2$ or $x\\ge 4.$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272210 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271915 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272210 1069 "q-141091" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272210 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272210 1071 "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2y\\operatorname{cot}⁡2x.\n$$\n\n Which of the following is a solution to this equation?\n\n- $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x$\n- $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+1$\n- $y=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272210 1072 #uuid "e106766c-2780-56ea-8754-563b11d222dd" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272210 1074 "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWe check each proposed solution by differentiating and substituting it into the original equation. \n\n- Differentiating $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x,$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2y\\operatorname{cot}⁡2x \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x & \\overset{?}{=}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\cdot \\operatorname{cot}⁡2x \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true*. Therefore, the function $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+1,$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2y\\operatorname{cot}⁡2x \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x & \\overset{?}{=}2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+1)\\operatorname{cot}⁡2x \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x & \\overset{?}{=}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+2\\operatorname{cot}⁡2x \\\\ 0 & =2\\operatorname{cot}⁡2x.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+1$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x,$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2y\\operatorname{cot}⁡2x \\\\ -6\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x & \\overset{?}{=}2(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)\\cdot \\operatorname{cot}⁡2x \\\\ -6\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x & =-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true*. Therefore, the function $y=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272211 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271797 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272211 1069 "q-141092" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272211 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272211 1071 "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-y\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.\n$$\n\n Which of the following is a solution to this equation?\n\n- $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$\n- $y=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$\n- $y=-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272211 1072 #uuid "e6e3bd06-ef25-5819-bfee-624e8862d278" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272211 1074 "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWe check each proposed solution by differentiating and substituting it into the original equation. \n\n- Differentiating $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-y\\operatorname{tan}⁡x \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & \\overset{?}{=}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot \\operatorname{tan}⁡x \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true*. Therefore, the function $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-y\\operatorname{tan}⁡x \\\\ -3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & \\overset{?}{=}-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot \\operatorname{tan}⁡x \\\\ -3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true*. Therefore, the function $y=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-y\\operatorname{tan}⁡x \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & \\overset{?}{=}-(-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\cdot \\operatorname{tan}⁡x \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true*. Therefore, the function $y=-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272212 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271782 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272212 1069 "q-141093" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272212 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272212 1071 "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2}{{e}^{y/2}}.\n$$\n\n Which of the following is a solution to this equation?\n\n- $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$\n- $y=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$\n- $y=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272212 1072 #uuid "8c4f3444-d6eb-515b-bbe6-1939e7e4a2dd" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272212 1074 "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWe check each proposed solution by differentiating and substituting it into the original equation. \n\n- Differentiating $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2}{{e}^{y/2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2}{{e}^{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)/2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2}{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\sqrt{x}}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{x} & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{x}} \\\\ 1 & =2\\sqrt{x}.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2}{x}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2}{{e}^{y/2}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2}{{e}^{(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)/2}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2}{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{x} & =\\frac{2}{x}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true.* Therefore, the function $y=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{x}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2}{{e}^{y/2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2}{{e}^{(-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)/2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2}{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{-1/2})}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2}{{x}^{-1/2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{x} & =2\\sqrt{x} \\\\ -1 & =2x\\sqrt{x}.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272213 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271761 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272213 1069 "q-141098" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272213 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272213 1071 "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{4y}{x},\\,x\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n Which of the following is a solution to this equation?\n\n- $y=4x$\n- $y=2{x}^{2}$\n- $y={x}^{3}$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272213 1072 #uuid "fbaad5c7-c732-5938-9213-ace2aa1f23df" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272213 1074 "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWe check each proposed solution by differentiating and substituting it into the original equation. \n\n- Differentiating $y=4x,$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=4.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{4y}{x} \\\\ 4 & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{4(4x)}{x} \\\\ 4 & \\ne 16.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *false*. Therefore, the function $y=4x$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=2{x}^{2},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=4x.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{4y}{x} \\\\ 4x & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{4(2{x}^{2})}{x} \\\\ 4x & \\overset{?}{=}8x \\\\ -4x & \\ne 0.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y=2{x}^{2}$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y={x}^{3},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=3{x}^{2}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{4y}{x} \\\\ 3{x}^{2} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{4({x}^{3})}{x} \\\\ 3{x}^{2} & \\ne 4{x}^{2} \\\\ 0 & \\ne {x}^{2}.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y={x}^{3}$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None.\"" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272214 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271678 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272214 1069 "q-141099" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272214 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272214 1071 "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{2y}{x},\\,x\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n Which of the following is a solution to this equation?\n\n- $y=-\\frac{1}{x}$\n- $y=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}$\n- $y=\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}}$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272214 1072 #uuid "0b4e4e14-2aab-5c49-ba69-c1255e3d6145" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272214 1074 "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWe check each proposed solution by differentiating and substituting it into the original equation. \n\n- Differentiating $y=-\\frac{1}{x},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{2y}{x} \\\\ \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} & \\overset{?}{=}-\\frac{2(-\\frac{1}{x})}{x} \\\\ \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ 0 & \\ne \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y=-\\frac{1}{x}$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{2y}{x} \\\\ \\frac{2}{{x}^{3}} & \\overset{?}{=}-\\frac{2(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}{x} \\\\ \\frac{2}{{x}^{3}} & =\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true.* Therefore, the function $y=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{8}{{x}^{3}}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{2y}{x} \\\\ -\\frac{8}{{x}^{3}} & \\overset{?}{=}-\\frac{2(\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}})}{x} \\\\ -\\frac{8}{{x}^{3}} & =-\\frac{8}{{x}^{3}}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true.* Therefore, the function $y=\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}}$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272215 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271791 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272215 1069 "q-141101" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272215 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272215 1071 "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{5y}{x},\\,x\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n Which of the following is a solution to this equation?\n\n- $y={x}^{5}$\n- $y={x}^{10}$\n- $y=5{x}^{4}$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272215 1072 #uuid "1646b369-92a0-554a-b444-f79313d419b8" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272215 1074 "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWe check each proposed solution by differentiating and substituting it into the original equation. \n\n- Differentiating $y={x}^{5},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=5{x}^{4}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{5y}{x} \\\\ 5{x}^{4} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{5({x}^{5})}{x} \\\\ 5{x}^{4} & =5{x}^{4}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true*. Therefore, the function $y={x}^{5}$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y={x}^{10},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=10{x}^{9}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{5y}{x} \\\\ 10{x}^{9} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{5({x}^{10})}{x} \\\\ 10{x}^{9} & \\overset{?}{=}5{x}^{9} \\\\ 5{x}^{9} & \\ne 0.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y={x}^{10}$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=5{x}^{4},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=20{x}^{3}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{5y}{x} \\\\ 20{x}^{3} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{5(5{x}^{4})}{x} \\\\ 20{x}^{3} & \\overset{?}{=}25{x}^{3} \\\\ 0 & \\ne 5{x}^{3}.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y=5{x}^{4}$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272216 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271814 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272216 1069 "q-141103" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272216 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272216 1071 "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{{e}^{3y}}.\n$$\n\n Which of the following is a solution to this equation?\n\n- $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$\n- $y=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$\n- $y=-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272216 1072 #uuid "d8ec718b-9c17-5ad2-8af8-895d469a7090" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272216 1074 "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWe check each proposed solution by differentiating and substituting it into the original equation. \n\n- Differentiating $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{3y}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{1}{{e}^{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{1}{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3})}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{x} & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}} \\\\ {x}^{2} & =1.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{x}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{3y}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{1}{{e}^{3(-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{1}{{e}^{-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{1}{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{-3})}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{1}{{x}^{-3}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{x} & ={x}^{3} \\\\ -1 & ={x}^{4}.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{2}{x}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{3y}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{1}{{e}^{3(-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{1}{{e}^{-6\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{1}{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{-6})}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{1}{{x}^{-6}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{x} & ={x}^{6} \\\\ -2 & ={x}^{7}.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y=-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None.\"" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272217 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271766 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272217 1069 "q-141107" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272217 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272217 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{4y}{x},\\,y(-1)=-1.\n$$\n\nYou're given that $y=A{x}^{4}$ is the solution to this initial value problem. What is the value of the constant $A?$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272217 1072 #uuid "0e6c8b47-d479-53b1-8c84-47f5f3c59cd6" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272217 1074 "We're given that $y=A{x}^{4}$ is the solution to the initial value problem. Therefore, it must satisfy the differential equation *and* the initial condition $y(-1)=-1.$\n\nSubstituting the initial condition $y(-1)=-1$ into our solution and solving for $A,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =A{x}^{4} \\\\ -1 & =A(-1)^{4} \\\\ -1 & =A(1) \\\\ A & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=(-1){x}^{4}=-{x}^{4}.\n$$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272218 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271736 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272218 1069 "q-141108" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272218 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272218 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-y,\\,y(0)=1.\n$$\n\nYou're given that $y=A{e}^{-x}$ is the solution to this initial value problem. What is the value of the constant $A?$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272218 1072 #uuid "bcfaf845-ee85-5f89-a0ff-887be1bdd8e1" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272218 1074 "We're given that $y=A{e}^{-x}$ is the solution to the initial value problem. Therefore, it must satisfy the differential equation *and* the initial condition $y(0)=1.$\n\nSubstituting the initial condition $y(0)=1$ into our solution and solving for $A,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =A{e}^{-x} \\\\ 1 & =A{e}^{-0} \\\\ 1 & =A(1) \\\\ A & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=1{e}^{-x}={e}^{-x}.\n$$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272219 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271637 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272219 1069 "q-141109" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272219 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272219 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-2y,\\,y(0)=2.\n$$\n\nYou're given that $y=A{e}^{-2x}$ is the solution to this initial value problem. What is the value of the constant $A?$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272219 1072 #uuid "fca85355-bf58-5075-99c6-c35790d44507" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272219 1074 "We're given that $y=A{e}^{-2x}$ is the solution to the initial value problem. Therefore, it must satisfy the differential equation *and* the initial condition $y(0)=2.$\n\nSubstituting the initial condition $y(0)=2$ into our solution and solving for $A,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =A{e}^{-2x} \\\\ 2 & =A{e}^{-2(0)} \\\\ 2 & =A{e}^{0} \\\\ 2 & =A(1) \\\\ A & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=2{e}^{-2x}.\n$$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272220 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271648 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272220 1069 "q-141111" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272220 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272220 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-2y\\operatorname{tan}⁡2x,\\,y(\\frac{\\pi }{8})=\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nYou're given that $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x$ is the solution to this initial value problem. What is the value of the constant $A?$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272220 1072 #uuid "42cc7718-b801-5574-add3-311d9f7972bc" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272220 1074 "We're given that $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x$ is the solution to the initial value problem. Therefore, it must satisfy the differential equation *and* the initial condition $y(\\frac{\\pi }{8})=\\sqrt{2}.$\n\nSubstituting the initial condition $y(\\frac{\\pi }{8})=\\sqrt{2}$ into our solution and solving for $A,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & =A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{8}) \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & =A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & =A(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\ A & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x.\n$$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272221 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271677 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272221 1069 "q-141114" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272221 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272221 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{y}{x},\\,y(-1)=3.\n$$\n\nYou're given that $y=Ax$ is the solution to this initial value problem. What is the value of the constant $A?$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272221 1072 #uuid "9915548e-f09c-5476-a595-046194f74c54" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272221 1074 "We're given that $y=Ax$ is the solution to the initial value problem. Therefore, it must satisfy the differential equation *and* the initial condition $y(-1)=3.$\n\nSubstituting the initial condition $y(-1)=3$ into our solution and solving for $A,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =Ax \\\\ 3 & =A(-1) \\\\ 3 & =-A \\\\ A & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=-3x.\n$$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272222 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271956 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272222 1069 "q-141131" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272222 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272222 1071 "Which of the following is equivalent to \n${\\int }_{0}^{4}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{16-{x}^{2}}?$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272222 1072 #uuid "4210ae79-bd0f-5b16-abae-9f823d727152" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272222 1074 "This is an improper integral of the second kind because the function under the integral is unbounded as we approach the *upper* integration limit from any point that lies in the interval $x\\in (0,4):$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{16-{x}^{2}}\\to \\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to {4}^{-}\n$$\n\nWe can attempt to evaluate this integral by integrating $f(x)$ over the interval $x\\in [0,b]$ and taking the limit as $b\\to {4}^{-}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{4}\\frac{1}{16-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{b\\to {4}^{-}}({\\int }_{0}^{b}\\frac{1}{16-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272223 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271636 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272223 1069 "q-141132" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272223 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272223 1071 "Which of the following is equivalent to \n${\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{x(x+1)}?$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272223 1072 #uuid "f97f77ec-4343-516d-880c-87291b994cbc" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272223 1074 "This is an improper integral of the second kind because the function under the integral is unbounded as we approach the *lower* integration limit from any point that lies in the interval $x\\in (0,2):$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{x(x+1)}\\to \\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to {0}^{+}\n$$\n\nWe can attempt to evaluate this integral by integrating $f(x)$ over the interval $x\\in [a,2]$ and taking the limit as $a\\to {0}^{+}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{x(x+1)}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}({\\int }_{a}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{x(x+1)}\\,)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272224 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271727 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272224 1069 "q-141133" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272224 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272224 1071 "![](images/33/33bec46293f976bfb829e987cd3ce26fa5cc67310bc830fc8f46b7f0d57b3acf.png)\n\nA part of the graph of a polynomial $p(x)$ is given above. Which of the following statements i