e root of even multiplicity." 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272228 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271756 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272228 1069 "q-141148" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272228 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272228 1071 "What is the coefficient matrix for the following system of linear equations?\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}3x-2y=0 \\\\ 5x+y=1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272228 1072 #uuid "da98e9ed-f51b-5bf7-a799-c464ff080850" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272228 1074 "The coefficient matrix consists of the coefficients of the variables: \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272229 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271580 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272229 1069 "q-141150" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272229 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272229 1071 "What is the coefficient matrix for the following system of linear equations?\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-x+2y=0 \\\\ -5x-3y=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272229 1072 #uuid "15d79f2e-48ef-515e-9b09-89751be9b1ba" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272229 1074 "The coefficient matrix consists of the coefficients of the variables: \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ -5 & -3\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272230 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271650 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272230 1069 "q-141154" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272230 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272230 1071 "What is the coefficient matrix for the following system of linear equations?\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2x+y=0 \\\\ x+9y=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272230 1072 #uuid "d72e4600-7dd6-5524-8f3a-bd16eaec9c41" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272230 1074 "The coefficient matrix consists of the coefficients of the variables: \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 9\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272231 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271664 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272231 1069 "q-141155" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272231 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272231 1071 "What is the coefficient matrix for the following system of linear equations?\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-x+5y=2 \\\\ 2x+y=1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272231 1072 #uuid "41e58fa0-6c8f-5e48-aa13-b6564632b7cb" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272231 1074 "The coefficient matrix consists of the coefficients of the variables: \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 5 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272232 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271749 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272232 1069 "q-141159" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272232 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272232 1071 "Consider the standard Hermitian inner product $⟨\\,\\cdot \\,,\\,\\cdot \\,⟩$ over $C.$ \nWhich of the following statements are true? \n\n- $⟨a,b⟩=⟨b,a⟩$ for any $a,b\\in {C}^{n}$\n- $⟨a,\\alpha b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩$ for any $a,b\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in R$\n- $⟨a,a⟩>0$ for any $a\\in {C}^{n}$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272232 1072 #uuid "525bde81-7550-5f13-8e28-5c16a04ee9f0" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272232 1074 "Recall that the standard Hermitian inner product over $C$ satisfies the following properties:\n\n- It is linear in the first argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a+b,c⟩=⟨a,c⟩+⟨b,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨\\alpha a,b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It is antilinear in the second argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b+c⟩=⟨a,b⟩+⟨a,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨a,\\alpha b⟩=\\overset{―}{\\alpha }⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It has conjugate symmetry. For any $a,b\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b⟩=\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}.$\n\n- It is positive-definite. For any $a\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,a⟩$ is a non-negative real number, and $⟨a,a⟩=0$ if and only if $a=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. The inner product over $C$ has conjugate symmetry.\n\n- Statement II is true. For any $\\alpha \\in R,$ we have $\\overset{―}{\\alpha }=\\alpha .$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n⟨a,\\alpha b⟩=\\overset{―}{\\alpha }⟨a,b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Notice that if $a=0,$ then $⟨a,a⟩=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272233 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271700 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272233 1069 "q-141160" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272233 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272233 1071 "Consider the standard Hermitian inner product $⟨\\,\\cdot \\,,\\,\\cdot \\,⟩$ over $C.$ \nWhich of the following statements are true? \n\n- $⟨ia,b⟩=i⟨a,b⟩$ for any $a,b\\in {C}^{n}$\n- $⟨a,\\overset{―}{\\alpha }b⟩=⟨\\alpha a,b⟩$ for any $a,b\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C$\n- $⟨ia,a⟩\\in R$ for any $a\\in {C}^{n}$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272233 1072 #uuid "396a82c3-ba69-5d62-8d39-94fffd303517" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272233 1074 "Recall that the standard Hermitian inner product over $C$ satisfies the following properties:\n\n- It is linear in the first argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a+b,c⟩=⟨a,c⟩+⟨b,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨\\alpha a,b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It is antilinear in the second argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b+c⟩=⟨a,b⟩+⟨a,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨a,\\alpha b⟩=\\overset{―}{\\alpha }⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It has conjugate symmetry. For any $a,b\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b⟩=\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}.$\n\n- It is positive-definite. For any $a\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,a⟩$ is a non-negative real number, and $⟨a,a⟩=0$ if and only if $a=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is true.  Indeed, the inner product in ${C}^{n}$ is linear in the first argument. So, a constant multiple from the first argument can be factored out.\n\n- Statement II is true. By the antilinearity of a complex inner product in the second argument and linearity in the first argument, we have \n\n$$\n⟨a,\\overset{―}{\\alpha }b⟩=\\overset{―}{(\\overset{―}{\\alpha })}⟨a,b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩=⟨\\alpha a,b⟩.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Notice that $⟨a,a⟩\\in R$ for any $a\\in {C}^{n}.$ Therefore, if $a\\ne 0,$ then\n\n$$\n⟨ia,a⟩=i⟨a,a⟩\\notin R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272234 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271842 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272234 1069 "q-141161" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272234 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272234 1071 "Given that $⟨\\,\\cdot \\,,\\,\\cdot \\,⟩$ is the standard Hermitian inner product over $C,$ find $⟨2a+3b,\\,c⟩$ if \n\n$\\,$ $⟨c,a⟩=2-\\text{i}\\,$ and $\\,⟨b,c⟩=1+2\\text{i}.$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272234 1072 #uuid "6190711d-7ba0-5244-bc34-0cc4e9ad3523" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272234 1074 "Recall that the inner product over $C$ satisfies the following properties. \n\n- It is linear in the first argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a+b,c⟩=⟨a,c⟩+⟨b,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨\\alpha a,b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It is antilinear in the second argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b+c⟩=⟨a,b⟩+⟨a,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨a,\\alpha b⟩=\\overset{―}{\\alpha }⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It has conjugate symmetry. For any $a,b\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b⟩=\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}.$\n\n- It is positive-definite. For any $a\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,a⟩$ is a non-negative real number, and $⟨a,a⟩=0$ if and only if $a=0.$\n\nFirst, we use the fact that the inner product over $C$ is linear in the first argument: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨2a+3b,\\,c⟩ & =⟨2a,c⟩+⟨3b,c⟩ \\\\  & =2⟨a,c⟩+3⟨b,c⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we use the fact that the inner product over $C$ has conjugate symmetry: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2⟨a,c⟩+3⟨b,c⟩ & =2\\overset{―}{⟨c,a⟩}+3⟨b,c⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute the values for $⟨c,a⟩$ and $⟨b,c⟩:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\overset{―}{⟨c,a⟩}+3⟨b,c⟩ & =2(\\overset{―}{2-\\text{i}})+3(1+2\\text{i}) \\\\  & =2(2+\\text{i})+3(1+2\\text{i}) \\\\  & =4+2\\text{i}+3+6\\text{i} \\\\  & =7+8\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272235 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271695 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272235 1069 "q-141162" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272235 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272235 1071 "Given that $⟨\\,\\cdot \\,,\\,\\cdot \\,⟩$ is the standard Hermitian inner product over $C,$ find $⟨a,\\,2b+(3-2\\text{i})c⟩$ if \n\n$\\,$ $⟨b,a⟩=1-\\text{i}\\,$ and $\\,⟨a,c⟩=3\\text{i}.$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272235 1072 #uuid "dc64db90-b850-561a-9a61-2d8c00a877b1" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272235 1074 "Recall that the inner product over $C$ satisfies the following properties. \n\n- It is linear in the first argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a+b,c⟩=⟨a,c⟩+⟨b,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨\\alpha a,b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It is antilinear in the second argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b+c⟩=⟨a,b⟩+⟨a,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨a,\\alpha b⟩=\\overset{―}{\\alpha }⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It has conjugate symmetry. For any $a,b\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b⟩=\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}.$\n\n- It is positive-definite. For any $a\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,a⟩$ is a non-negative real number, and $⟨a,a⟩=0$ if and only if $a=0.$\n\nFirst, we use the fact that the inner product over $C$ is antilinear in the second argument:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨a,\\,2b+(3-2\\text{i})c⟩ & =⟨a,2b⟩+⟨a,(3-2\\text{i})c⟩ \\\\  & =\\overset{―}{2}⟨a,b⟩+(\\overset{―}{3-2\\text{i}})⟨a,c⟩ \\\\  & =2⟨a,b⟩+(3+2\\text{i})⟨a,c⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that the inner product over $C$ has conjugate symmetry:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2⟨a,b⟩+(3+2\\text{i})⟨a,c⟩ & =2\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}+(3+2\\text{i})⟨a,c⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute the values for $⟨b,a⟩$ and $⟨a,c⟩:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}+(3+2\\text{i})⟨a,c⟩ & =2\\cdot \\overset{―}{1-\\text{i}}+(3+2\\text{i})\\cdot (3\\text{i}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot (1+\\text{i})-6+9\\text{i} \\\\  & =-4+11\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272236 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271903 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272236 1069 "q-141167" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272236 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272236 1071 "Let $V={R}_{2}[t]$ be the vector space of all polynomials of degree at most $2,$ equipped with the inner product\n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩=x(1)y(1)+x(2)y(2)+x(3)y(3).\n$$\n\nCalculate the norm of the vector $p(t)=2-t+{t}^{2}.$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272236 1072 #uuid "5af30cbe-62d3-5504-84b8-034b67c250b5" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272236 1074 "The norm of a vector $p(t)$ with respect to an inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖p(t)‖=\\sqrt{⟨p(t),p(t)⟩}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $p(t)=2-t+{t}^{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\np(1)=2,\\,p(2)=4,\\,p(3)=8.\n$$\n\nComputing $‖p(t)‖$ using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖p(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{⟨p(t),p(t)⟩} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{p(1)p(1)+p(2)p(2)+p(3)p(3)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{[p(1)]^{2}+[p(2)]^{2}+[p(3)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{4}^{2}+{8}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+16+64} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{84} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{21}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272237 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271596 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272237 1069 "q-141169" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272237 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272237 1071 "Let $V={R}_{2}[t]$ be the vector space of all polynomials of degree at most $2,$ equipped with the inner product\n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩=x(1)y(1)+x(0)y(0)+x(-1)y(-1).\n$$\n\nCalculate the norm of the vector $p(t)=2-3t+{t}^{2}.$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272237 1072 #uuid "3309591e-3720-524d-a18f-2c18b47f52ac" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272237 1074 "The norm of a vector $p(t)$ with respect to an inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖p(t)‖=\\sqrt{⟨p(t),p(t)⟩}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $p(t)=2-3t+{t}^{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\np(1)=0,\\,p(0)=2,\\,p(-1)=6.\n$$\n\nComputing $‖p(t)‖$ using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖p(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{⟨p(t),p(t)⟩} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{p(1)p(1)+p(0)p(0)+p(-1)p(-1)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{[p(1)]^{2}+[p(0)]^{2}+[p(-1)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{6}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{0+4+36} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{40} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272238 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271920 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272238 1069 "q-141170" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272238 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272238 1071 "Let $V=C[-1,1]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[-1,1],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{-1}^{1}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nCalculate the norm of the vector $f(t)=1-t.$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272238 1072 #uuid "4c4a016c-b4a0-506e-993b-378d9e3fc8f3" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272238 1074 "The norm of a vector $f(t)$ with respect to an inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ is defined as\n\n$$\n‖f(t)‖=\\sqrt{⟨f(t),f(t)⟩}.\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(t),f(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(f(t))^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(1-t)^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}({t}^{2}-2t+1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={(\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}-{t}^{2}+t)|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{3}-1+1)-(\\frac{(-1)^{3}}{3}-(-1)^{2}+(-1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n‖f(t)‖=\\sqrt{\\frac{8}{3}}=2\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}}.\n$$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272239 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271600 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272239 1069 "q-141171" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272239 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272239 1071 "Let $V=C[-\\pi ,\\pi ]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[-\\pi ,\\pi ],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nCalculate the norm of the vector $f(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272239 1072 #uuid "4537b658-e8b9-5fe5-89e3-2038e8f94ba3" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272239 1074 "The norm of a vector $f(t)$ with respect to an inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ is defined as\n\n$$\n‖f(t)‖=\\sqrt{⟨f(t),f(t)⟩}.\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(t),f(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }(f(t))^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\,{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\,({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(t+\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t}{2}){|}_{-\\pi }^{\\pi } \\\\  & =(\\pi +\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\pi }{2})-((-\\pi )+\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-2\\pi )}{2}) \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we made use of the trigonometric identities\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t=1\\,\\text{and}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\n$$\n\nFinally then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖f(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{2\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272240 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271815 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272240 1069 "q-141174" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272240 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272240 1071 "An inner product on $V={R}^{3}$ is defined as $⟨x,y⟩={x}_{1}{y}_{1}+3{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+{x}_{3}{y}_{3}.$ \n\nNormalize the  vector  $v=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ under this inner product." 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272240 1072 #uuid "afb4bbee-f88e-55e1-b82e-818f2d9c1c71" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272240 1074 "The norm of a vector $v$ with respect to an inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖=\\sqrt{⟨v,v⟩}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨v,v⟩ & =2\\cdot 2+3\\cdot 1\\cdot 1+(-1)\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =4+3+1 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖ & =\\sqrt{8}=2\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo normalize the vector $v$, we divide $v$ by its norm $‖v‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =\\frac{v}{‖v‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272241 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271927 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272241 1069 "q-141175" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272241 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272241 1071 "Let $V=C[0,1]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[0,1],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{1}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nNormalize the vector $f(t)=t-{t}^{2}.$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272241 1072 #uuid "a9c89d04-85a8-589e-8ec5-f398a5881a68" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272241 1074 "The norm of a vector $f(t)$ with respect to an inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ is defined as\n\n$$\n‖f(t)‖=\\sqrt{⟨f(t),f(t)⟩}.\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(t),f(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{(t-{t}^{2})}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,({t}^{2}-2{t}^{3}+{t}^{4}),\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={(\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{{t}^{4}}{2}+\\frac{{t}^{5}}{5})|}_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{30}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n‖f(t)‖=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{30}}.\n$$\n\nTo normalize the vector $f(t),$ we divide $f(t)$ by its norm $‖f(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & =\\frac{f(t)}{‖f(t)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{(t-{t}^{2})}{\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{30}}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{30}(t-{t}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272242 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271768 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272242 1069 "q-141181" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272242 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272242 1071 "Which of the following is equivalent to \n${\\int }_{-1}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{4-{x}^{2}}?$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272242 1072 #uuid "7a18bf49-a00f-501d-8365-1640e7596df7" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272242 1074 "This is an improper integral of the second kind because the function under the integral is unbounded as we approach the *upper* integration limit from any point that lies in the interval $x\\in (-1,2):$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{4-{x}^{2}}\\to \\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to {2}^{-}\n$$\n\nWe can attempt to evaluate this integral by integrating $f(x)$ over the interval $x\\in [-1,b]$ and taking the limit as $b\\to {2}^{-}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{4-{x}^{2}} & ={lim}_{b\\to {2}^{-}}({\\int }_{-1}^{b}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{4-{x}^{2}})\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272243 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271895 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272243 1069 "q-141192" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272243 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272243 1071 "$$\nA\\text{ is invertible}\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{n} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))==\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the equivalence above, where $A$ is an $n\\,\\times \\,n$ matrix. Which of the following should be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272243 1072 #uuid "a09c603d-a3ea-5c23-b944-39ca22d1abd0" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272243 1074 "Recall that the *invertible matrix theorem* states the following: \n\n*Let $A$ be an $n\\,\\times \\,n$ matrix. Then we have the following equivalence:*\n\n$$\nA\\text{ is invertible}\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{n} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=n=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is $n.$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272244 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271606 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272244 1069 "q-141199" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272244 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272244 1071 "Which of the following statements are true, given that $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix and $v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ is a solution of the equation $Ax=0?$\n\n- $\\text{Null}(A)={0}$\n- $A$ is not invertible\n- $\\text{Col}(A)\\ne {R}^{4}$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272244 1072 #uuid "f63719f4-7a1e-5b43-8302-7786de4d0619" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272244 1074 "Since $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{4} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=4=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn. \n\n- Statement I is false. Since $v\\ne 0$ and $Av=0,$ \nthen $v\\in \\text{Null}(A)$ and, therefore, $\\text{Null}(A)\\ne {0}.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $\\text{Null}(A)\\ne {0},$ then according to the invertible matrix theorem, the matrix $A$ can't be invertible.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $A$ is not invertible, then by the invertible matrix theorem, we must have $\\text{Col}(A)\\ne {R}^{4}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272245 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271780 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272245 1069 "q-141200" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272245 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272245 1071 "Which of the following statements are true, given that $A$ is a $6\\times 6$ matrix and  $det(A)=-1?$ \n\n- $A$ is invertible\n- $\\text{Null}(A)={0}$\n- $\\text{nullity}(A)=6$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272245 1072 #uuid "8dad0d98-37c5-5af9-9c0a-4f0fabec2bcc" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272245 1074 "Since $A$ is a $6\\times 6$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{6} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=6=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn. \n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed,   a matrix $A$ is invertible if and only if $det(A)\\ne 0.$ Since here we have $det(A)=-1\\ne 0,$ our matrix $A$ must be  invertible.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, by the invertible matrix theorem\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,\\text{Null}(A)={0}.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $A$ is invertible,  it must be $\\text{nullity}(A)=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272246 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271871 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272246 1069 "q-141209" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272246 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272246 1071 "Which of the following is an improper integral of the second kind?\n\n- ${\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{{x}^{3}-8}\\,$  II. ${\\int }_{-2}^{1}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{{x}^{3}+8}\\,$  III. ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{{x}^{3}}$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272246 1072 #uuid "6a9082ef-b5c5-508a-ad30-c16427769a4a" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272246 1074 "Let's examine each of the given integrals.\n\n- Integral I is not an improper integral since it has finite limits and the function under the integral is finite at every point $x\\in [0,1].$\n\n- Integral II is an improper integral of the second kind since it has finite limits, and \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}+8}\\to \\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to (-2)^{+}.\n$$\n\n- Integral III is an improper integral over an *infinite* domain since its lower limit is infinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272247 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271841 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272247 1069 "q-141212" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272247 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272247 1071 "Which of the following is an improper integral of the second kind?\n\n- ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\sqrt{x}-1}\\,$  II. ${\\int }_{2}^{4}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{1-\\sqrt{x}}\\,$  III. ${\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\sqrt{x-1}}$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272247 1072 #uuid "5c6c3c56-7a5d-5137-ba4b-48f3b706f731" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272247 1074 "Let's examine each of the given integrals.\n\n- Integral I is an improper integral over an *infinite* domain since its upper limit is infinite.\n\n- Integral II is not an improper integral since it has finite limits and the function under the integral is finite at every point $x\\in [2,4].$\n\n- Integral III is an improper integral of the second kind since it has finite limits, and \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x-1}}\\to \\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to {1}^{+}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272248 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271675 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272248 1069 "q-141218" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272248 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272248 1071 "Which of the following is an improper integral of the second kind?\n\n- ${\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x\\,$  II. ${\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\infty }\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x\\,$  III. ${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272248 1072 #uuid "1c19bd8b-6180-555e-84ee-4d1df41e83f0" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272248 1074 "Let's examine each of the given integrals.\n\n- Integral I is an improper integral of the second kind since it has finite limits, and \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\to -\\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to {0}^{-}.\n$$\n\n- Integral II is an improper integral over an *infinite* domain since its upper limit is infinite.\n\n- Integral III is not an improper integral since it has finite limits and the function under the integral is finite at every point $x\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{4}].$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272249 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271941 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272249 1069 "q-141220" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272249 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272249 1071 "Consider a $8\\times 7$ matrix $A.$ If $\\text{dim(Null(}A))=2,$ then $\\text{rank}(A)=$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272249 1072 #uuid "e9ab814e-2358-5160-95c5-d43671e5affe" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272249 1074 "According to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A)=n.\n$$\n\nFor the given matrix, we have $n=7.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =7 \\\\ \\text{rank}(A)+2 & =7 \\\\ \\text{rank}(A) & =7-2 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272250 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271633 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272250 1069 "q-141225" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272250 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272250 1071 "Consider a $8\\times 10$ matrix $A.$ If $\\text{rank}(A)=6$ then $\\text{nullity}(A)=$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272250 1072 #uuid "43f25379-6ec0-5f21-88fe-048499052abb" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272250 1074 "According to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A)=n.\n$$\n\nFor the given matrix, we have $n=10.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ 6+\\text{nullity}(A) & =10 \\\\ \\text{nullity}(A) & =10-6 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272251 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271649 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272251 1069 "q-141228" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272251 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272251 1071 "Consider a $6\\times 8$ matrix $A.$ If $\\text{dim(Null(}A))=2,$ then $\\text{rank}(A)=$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272251 1072 #uuid "0934aa4b-cfbc-551f-aede-b568683fcc43" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272251 1074 "According to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A)=n.\n$$\n\nFor the given matrix, we have $n=8.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ \\text{rank}(A)+2 & =8 \\\\ \\text{rank}(A) & =8-2 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272252 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271877 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272252 1069 "q-141230" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272252 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272252 1071 "Let $A$ be a $5\\times 7$ matrix. If $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3},{b}_{4}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(A)$, find $\\text{dim(Null}(A)).$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272252 1072 #uuid "a8591660-5b8e-5e5d-b7c5-293cb529794e" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272252 1074 "According to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A)=n.\n$$\n\nFor the given matrix, we have $n=7,$ and since $B$ is formed by $4$ vectors, $\\text{rank}(A)=4.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ 4+\\text{nullity}(A) & =7 \\\\ \\text{nullity}(A) & =7-4 \\\\ \\text{dim(Null}(A)) & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272253 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271771 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272253 1069 "q-141234" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272253 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272253 1071 "Which of the following expressions are polynomials?\n\n- $(x)+(-{x}^{3}-1)$\n- $(2{x}^{4}-3)÷({x}^{2})$\n- $({x}^{2})-(2{x}^{5}+4x)$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272253 1072 #uuid "6faa06e9-6d50-5778-8c1a-50c6b3712803" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272253 1074 "Recall that polynomials have the following closure properties:\n\n- Polynomials are closed under addition, subtraction, and multiplication. This means that the sum, difference, and product of two polynomials is *always* a polynomial.\n\n- Polynomials are *not* closed under division. This means that the quotient of two polynomials is *not* always a polynomial.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our expressions in turn.\n\n- Expression I is a polynomial. It's a sum of two polynomials, and polynomials are closed under addition.\n\n- Expression II is *not* a polynomial. Carrying out the division, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2{x}^{4}-3}{{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{2{x}^{4}}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}-\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is *not* a polynomial.\n\n- Expression III gives a polynomial. It's a difference between two polynomials, and polynomials are closed under subtraction.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272254 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271581 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272254 1069 "q-141236" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272254 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272254 1071 "Which of the following expressions are polynomials?\n\n- $(4{x}^{6}-2)÷(2x)$\n- $(7x+1)\\cdot (2{x}^{3}+3)$\n- $({x}^{4}-3{x}^{2}+1)÷({x}^{2})$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272254 1072 #uuid "a6242aae-9378-55e8-b137-ddfe7f38d97f" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272254 1074 "Recall that polynomials have the following closure properties:\n\n- Polynomials are closed under addition, subtraction, and multiplication. This means that the sum, difference, and product of two polynomials is *always* a polynomial.\n\n- Polynomials are *not* closed under division. This means that the quotient of two polynomials is *not* always a polynomial.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our expressions in turn.\n\n- Expression I is *not* a polynomial. Carrying out the division, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4{x}^{6}-2}{2x} & =\\frac{4{x}^{6}}{2x}-\\frac{2}{2x} \\\\  & =2{x}^{5}-\\frac{1}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is *not* a polynomial.\n\n- Expression II gives a polynomial. It's a product of two polynomials, and polynomials are closed under multiplication.\n\n- Expression III is *not* a polynomial. Carrying out the division, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{4}-3{x}^{2}+1}{{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-3+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is *not* a polynomial.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272255 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271752 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272255 1069 "q-141240" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272255 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272255 1071 "Which of the following expressions are polynomials?\n\n- $(4{x}^{6}-x)+(3{x}^{5})$\n- $({x}^{3}-1)\\cdot (3-{x}^{2})$\n- $({x}^{3}-4{x}^{2})÷(x)$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272255 1072 #uuid "2a3f0c75-73fd-5349-840a-8899dcbd367a" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272255 1074 "Recall that polynomials have the following closure properties:\n\n- Polynomials are closed under addition, subtraction, and multiplication. This means that the sum, difference, and product of two polynomials is *always* a polynomial.\n\n- Polynomials are *not* closed under division. This means that the quotient of two polynomials is *not* always a polynomial.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our expressions in turn.\n\n- Expression I is a polynomial. It's a sum of two polynomials, and polynomials are closed under addition.\n\n- Expression II is a polynomial. It's a product of two polynomials, and polynomials are closed under multiplication.\n\n- Expression III is a polynomial. Even though polynomials are *not* closed under division, the quotient of these two polynomials results in a polynomial:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}^{3}-4{x}^{2})÷(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{3}-4{x}^{2}}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{x({x}^{2}-4x)}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{x}({x}^{2}-4x)}{\\cancel{x}} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-4x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272256 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271586 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272256 1069 "q-141245" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272256 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272256 1071 "Which of the following expressions are polynomials?\n\n- $(3{x}^{3}-2x)÷({x}^{3})$\n- $(10{x}^{6}-4{x}^{3})÷(2{x}^{3})$\n- $(2x-1)\\cdot ({x}^{3}+6x)$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272256 1072 #uuid "78e46443-6b0b-52be-80af-856b64b460d2" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272256 1074 "Recall that polynomials have the following closure properties:\n\n- Polynomials are closed under addition, subtraction, and multiplication. This means that the sum, difference, and product of two polynomials is *always* a polynomial.\n\n- Polynomials are *not* closed under division. This means that the quotient of two polynomials is *not* always a polynomial.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our expressions in turn.\n\n- Expression I is *not* a polynomial. Carrying out the division, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3{x}^{3}-2x}{{x}^{3}} & =\\frac{3{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{2x}{{x}^{3}} \\\\  & =3-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is *not* a polynomial.\n\n- Expression II is a polynomial. Even though polynomials are *not* closed under division, the quotient of these two polynomials results in a polynomial:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(10{x}^{6}-4{x}^{3})÷(2{x}^{3}) & =\\frac{10{x}^{6}-4{x}^{3}}{2{x}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{x}^{3}(5{x}^{3}-2)}{2{x}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{2{x}^{3}}(5{x}^{3}-2)}{\\cancel{2{x}^{3}}} \\\\  & =5{x}^{3}-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Expression III is a polynomial. It's a product of two polynomials, and polynomials are closed under multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272257 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271805 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272257 1069 "q-141250" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272257 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272257 1071 "If $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $4$ and $2$, respectively, what is the degree of $P\\cdot Q?$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272257 1072 #uuid "ad86116f-6da2-568f-94f0-ca321984855f" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272257 1074 "Since $P$ is a polynomial of degree $4,$ the leading term contains an ${x}^{4}.$\n\nSince $Q$ is a polynomial of degree $2,$ the leading term contains an ${x}^{2}.$\n\nIn $P\\cdot Q,$ the ${x}^{4}$ term in $P$ multiplies with the ${x}^{2}$ term in $Q$ to form a term containing ${x}^{4}\\cdot {x}^{2}={x}^{4+2}={x}^{6}.$\n\nTherefore, the degree of $P\\cdot Q$ must be $6.$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272258 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271880 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272258 1069 "q-141270" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272258 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272258 1071 "$\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+{135}^{∘})=$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272258 1072 #uuid "9a81f8fe-16ea-565d-8f32-af073a7db052" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272258 1074 "Recall the sum formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nApplying the sum formula for sine with $u=x$ and $v={135}^{∘},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+{135}^{∘}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡{135}^{∘}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡{135}^{∘} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2}+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272259 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271671 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272259 1069 "q-141271" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272259 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272259 1071 "$\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+{150}^{∘})=$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272259 1072 #uuid "9b662a6f-66f3-5ffb-bf0b-66a90ae7e592" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272259 1074 "Recall the sum formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nApplying the sum formula for sine with $u=x$ and $v={150}^{∘},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+{150}^{∘}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡{150}^{∘}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡{150}^{∘} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2}+\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272260 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271807 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272260 1069 "q-141275" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272260 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272260 1071 "$\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-{120}^{∘})=$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272260 1072 #uuid "b6e1f817-7c7e-51c8-bd09-e888759f9be3" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272260 1074 "Recall the difference formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nApplying the difference formula for sine with $u=x$ and $v={120}^{∘},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-{120}^{∘}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡{120}^{∘}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡{120}^{∘} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2}-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272261 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271626 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272261 1069 "q-141277" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272261 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272261 1071 "$\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-{150}^{∘})=$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272261 1072 #uuid "919084ee-61ae-56a2-a058-7e2ea993f757" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272261 1074 "Recall the difference formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nApplying the difference formula for sine with $u=x$ and $v={150}^{∘},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-{150}^{∘}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡{150}^{∘}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡{150}^{∘} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2}-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272262 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271862 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272262 1069 "q-141278" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272262 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272262 1071 "$\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-{180}^{∘})=$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272262 1072 #uuid "dd3a924d-681c-501c-bf69-7fbcdc5b6418" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272262 1074 "Recall the difference formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nApplying the difference formula for sine with $u=x$ and $v={180}^{∘},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-{180}^{∘}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡{180}^{∘}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡{180}^{∘} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot (-1)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot (0) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272263 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271925 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272263 1069 "q-141284" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272263 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533402 175921860455