operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272265 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271783 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272265 1069 "q-141312" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272265 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272265 1071 "Does the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{4{(-7)}^{n}}{{2}^{n}}$ for $n\\ge 1$ converge or diverge? If it converges, what is its limit?" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272265 1072 #uuid "28e36c28-53cf-5ecc-9bb5-73b0ad09588f" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272265 1074 "Suppose we have a geometric sequence with the first term ${a}_{1}$ and common ratio $r.$ Then, we have the following convergence properties:\n\n- if $|r|<1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to $0$\n\n- if $|r|>1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nMoreover, we have the following edge cases:\n\n- if $r=1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to ${a}_{1}$\n\n- if $r=-1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nFirst, we rewrite the sequence in the form ${a}_{n}=a\\cdot {r}^{n},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =\\frac{4{(-7)}^{n}}{{2}^{n}} \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{{(-7)}^{n}}{{2}^{n}} \\\\  & =4{(-\\frac{7}{2})}^{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now see that this is the geometric sequence with $r=-\\frac{7}{2}.$ \n\nSince $|r|>1,$ the sequence diverges." 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272266 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271943 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272266 1069 "q-141314" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272266 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272266 1071 "Does the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{8\\cdot {11}^{n}}{{9}^{n}}$ for $n\\ge 1$ converge or diverge? If it converges, what is its limit?" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272266 1072 #uuid "385046ae-9549-5d03-b987-27cdd162ac0c" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272266 1074 "Suppose we have a geometric sequence with the first term ${a}_{1}$ and common ratio $r.$ Then, we have the following convergence properties:\n\n- if $|r|<1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to $0$\n\n- if $|r|>1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nMoreover, we have the following edge cases:\n\n- if $r=1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to ${a}_{1}$\n\n- if $r=-1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nFirst, we rewrite the sequence in the form ${a}_{n}=a\\cdot {r}^{n},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =\\frac{8\\cdot {11}^{n}}{{9}^{n}} \\\\  & =8\\cdot \\frac{{11}^{n}}{{9}^{n}} \\\\  & =8{(\\frac{11}{9})}^{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now see that this is the geometric sequence with $r=\\frac{11}{9}.$ \n\nSince $|r|>1,$ the sequence diverges." 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272267 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271893 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272267 1069 "q-141318" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272267 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272267 1071 "Consider the geometric sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{1}{x}{(2x)}^{n},$ where $x$ is a positive real number and $n\\ge 1.$ What are the values of $x$ for which the sequence converges?" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272267 1072 #uuid "2f5ee048-8615-566b-926f-e3dcfb93f14a" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272267 1074 "Suppose we have a geometric sequence with the first term ${a}_{1}$ and common ratio $r.$ Then, we have the following convergence properties:\n\n- if $|r|<1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to $0$\n\n- if $|r|>1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nMoreover, we have the following edge cases:\n\n- if $r=1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to ${a}_{1}$\n\n- if $r=-1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nThe given sequence is a geometric sequence with the common ratio $r=2x.$ \nSince $x$ is positive,  $r$ is also positive, So, the convergence condition is $0<r\\le 1.$ \n\nTherefore, the  sequence converges for \n\n$$\n0<2x\\le 1\\,⟹\\,0<x\\le \\frac{1}{2}.\n$$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272268 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271758 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272268 1069 "q-141322" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272268 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272268 1071 "Consider the sequence ${a}_{n}=8{(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4})}^{n},$ where $x$ is a positive real number and $n\\ge 1.$ What are the values of $x$ for which the sequence converges?" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272268 1072 #uuid "cba8d40e-9ff0-576c-b4c2-5a73d8552590" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272268 1074 "Suppose we have a geometric sequence with the first term ${a}_{1}$ and common ratio $r.$ Then, we have the following convergence properties:\n\n- if $|r|<1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to $0$\n\n- if $|r|>1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nMoreover, we have the following edge cases:\n\n- if $r=1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to ${a}_{1}$\n\n- if $r=-1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nThis given sequence is a geometric sequence with common ratio $r=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}.$ Since ${x}^{2}$ is positive, $r$ is also positive, So, the convergence condition is $0<r\\le 1.$ \n\nTherefore, the  sequence converges if \n\n$$\n0<\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}\\le 1\\,⟹\\,0<{x}^{2}\\le 4.\n$$\n\nTaking into account the fact that $x$ is positive, we get\n\n$$\n0<x\\le 2.\n$$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272269 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271861 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272269 1069 "q-141323" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272269 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272269 1071 "Consider the geometric sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{x}{4}{(\\frac{8}{x})}^{n},$ where $x$ is a positive real number and $n\\ge 1.$ What are the values of $x$ for which the sequence converges?" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272269 1072 #uuid "342f5e64-a399-5f8b-a691-0fd529d174f8" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272269 1074 "Suppose we have a geometric sequence with the first term ${a}_{1}$ and common ratio $r.$ Then, we have the following convergence properties:\n\n- if $|r|<1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to $0$\n\n- if $|r|>1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nMoreover, we have the following edge cases:\n\n- if $r=1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to ${a}_{1}$\n\n- if $r=-1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nThe given sequence is a geometric sequence with the common ratio $r=\\frac{8}{x}.$ \nSince $x$ is positive,  $r$ is also positive, So, the convergence condition is $0<r\\le 1.$ \n\nTherefore, the  sequence converges for \n\n$$\n0<\\frac{8}{x}\\le 1\\,⟹\\,x\\ge 8.\n$$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272270 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271958 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272270 1069 "q-141329" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272270 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272270 1071 "Consider the geometric sequence ${a}_{n}=2x{(\\frac{16}{{x}^{4}})}^{n},$ where $x$ is a positive real number and $n\\ge 1.$ What are the values of $x$ for which the sequence converges?" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272270 1072 #uuid "c6a32232-02fb-5219-8889-a984024d7a02" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272270 1074 "Suppose we have a geometric sequence with the first term ${a}_{1}$ and common ratio $r.$ Then, we have the following convergence properties:\n\n- if $|r|<1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to $0$\n\n- if $|r|>1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nMoreover, we have the following edge cases:\n\n- if $r=1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to ${a}_{1}$\n\n- if $r=-1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nThe given sequence is a geometric sequence with the common ratio $r=\\frac{16}{{x}^{4}}.$ \nSince ${x}^{4}$ is positive,  $r$ is also positive, So, the convergence condition is $0<r\\le 1.$ \n\nTherefore, the  sequence converges for \n\n$$\n0<\\frac{16}{{x}^{4}}\\le 1\\,⟹\\,{x}^{4}\\ge 16.\n$$\n\nTaking into account the fact that $x$ is positive, we get\n\n$$\nx\\ge 2.\n$$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272271 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271770 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272271 1069 "q-141332" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272271 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272271 1071 "Consider the geometric sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{x}{2}{(\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}})}^{n},$ where $x$ is a positive real number and $n\\ge 1.$ What are the values of $x$ for which the sequence converges?" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272271 1072 #uuid "df355f69-884b-5a24-91f1-5877dbb36e55" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272271 1074 "Suppose we have a geometric sequence with the first term ${a}_{1}$ and common ratio $r.$ Then, we have the following convergence properties:\n\n- if $|r|<1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to $0$\n\n- if $|r|>1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nMoreover, we have the following edge cases:\n\n- if $r=1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to ${a}_{1}$\n\n- if $r=-1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nThe given sequence is a geometric sequence with the common ratio $r=\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}.$ \nSince ${x}^{2}$ is positive,  $r$ is also positive, So, the convergence condition is $0<r\\le 1.$ \n\nTherefore, the  sequence converges for \n\n$$\n0<\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}\\le 1\\,⟹\\,{x}^{2}\\ge 2.\n$$\n\n \n\nTaking into account the fact that $x$ is positive, we get\n\n$$\nx\\ge \\sqrt{2}.\n$$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272272 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271739 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272272 1069 "q-141336" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272272 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272272 1071 "Consider the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{{x}^{2n}}{{16}^{n+1}},$ where $x$ is a positive real number and $n\\ge 1.$ What are the values of $x$ for which the sequence converges?" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272272 1072 #uuid "b45c8d5b-a164-5f47-b24d-aad447830fc5" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272272 1074 "Suppose we have a geometric sequence with the first term ${a}_{1}$ and common ratio $r.$ Then, we have the following convergence properties:\n\n- if $|r|<1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to $0$\n\n- if $|r|>1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nMoreover, we have the following edge cases:\n\n- if $r=1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to ${a}_{1}$\n\n- if $r=-1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nFirst, we rewrite the sequence in the form ${a}_{n}=a\\cdot {r}^{n},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =\\frac{{x}^{2n}}{{16}^{n+1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2n}}{16\\cdot {16}^{n}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16}\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{2n}}{{16}^{n}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16}\\cdot {(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16})}^{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe see that this is a geometric sequence with the common ratio $r=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}.$ Since ${x}^{2}$ is positive, $r$ is also positive.  So, the convergence condition is $0<r\\le 1.$ \n\nTherefore, the  sequence converges for\n\n$$\n0<\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}\\le 1\\,⟹\\,0<{x}^{2}\\le 16.\n$$\n\nTaking into account that $x$ is positive, we get\n\n$$\n0<x\\le 4.\n$$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272273 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271523 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272273 1069 "q-141344" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272273 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272273 1071 "$$\nA\\text{ is }\\text{ }\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{n} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=n=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the equivalence above, where $A$ is an $n\\,\\times \\,n$ matrix. Which of the following should be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272273 1072 #uuid "828b3c9d-913c-55f0-bf08-423c9d84e9d3" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272273 1074 "Recall that the *invertible matrix theorem* states the following: \n\n*Let $A$ be an $n\\,\\times \\,n$ matrix. Then we have the following equivalence:*\n\n$$\nA\\text{ is }\\text{invertible}\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{n} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=n=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is \"invertible\"." 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272274 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271885 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272274 1069 "q-141357" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272274 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272274 1071 "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡u=\\frac{8}{17},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡v=\\frac{4}{5},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡v=\\frac{3}{5}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $u$ lies in the first quadrant, find the value of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v).$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272274 1072 #uuid "aa20e82e-b00e-53d9-aae3-c53c2db1c9a1" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272274 1074 "First, recall the difference formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡u$, $\\operatorname{sin}⁡v$, and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡v.$ To find the missing value of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡u$, we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡u+{cos}^{2}⁡u=1\\,⟹\\,{cos}^{2}⁡u=1-{sin}^{2}⁡u\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡u & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡u \\\\  & =1-{(\\frac{8}{17})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{8}^{2}}{{17}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{64}{289} \\\\  & =\\frac{289}{289}-\\frac{64}{289} \\\\  & =\\frac{225}{289}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{225}{289}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{15}{17}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $u$ lies in the first quadrant, where cosine is positive. So, we disregard the negative value, and we have \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡u=\\frac{15}{17}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the difference formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =(\\frac{8}{17})(\\frac{3}{5})-(\\frac{15}{17})(\\frac{4}{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{24}{85}-\\frac{60}{85} \\\\  & =-\\frac{36}{85}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272275 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271935 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272275 1069 "q-141360" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272275 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272275 1071 "Find the exact value of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-\\frac{\\pi }{12}).$\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $-\\frac{\\pi }{12}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}-\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$*" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272275 1072 #uuid "7f15816e-a4ea-5e54-ac29-0fac8d7bc4b0" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272275 1074 "Recall the difference formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\n$$\n\nApplying the difference formula for cosine with $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ and $y=\\frac{\\pi }{3},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-\\frac{\\pi }{12}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}-\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{6}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272276 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271640 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272276 1069 "q-141364" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272276 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272276 1071 "$\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+{30}^{∘})=$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272276 1072 #uuid "b5eb1983-1627-51b5-a21b-a1c11d629a2e" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272276 1074 "First, we recall the sum formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nApplying the sum formula for cosine with $u=x$ and $v={30}^{∘}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+{30}^{∘}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡{30}^{∘}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡{30}^{∘} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272277 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271566 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272277 1069 "q-141365" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272277 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272277 1071 "$\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+{60}^{∘})=$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272277 1072 #uuid "48b60017-29fb-5b57-99c2-0a9425be35c5" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272277 1074 "First, we recall the sum formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nApplying the sum formula for cosine with $u=x$ and $v={60}^{∘}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+{60}^{∘}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡{60}^{∘}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡{60}^{∘} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272278 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271541 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272278 1069 "q-141366" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272278 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272278 1071 "$\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+{90}^{∘})=$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272278 1072 #uuid "65040572-658e-531f-bad2-ae30b6cfee15" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272278 1074 "First, we recall the sum formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nApplying the sum formula for cosine with $u=x$ and $v={90}^{∘}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+{90}^{∘}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡{90}^{∘}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡{90}^{∘} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot 0-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272279 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271952 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272279 1069 "q-141374" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272279 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272279 1071 "$\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-{150}^{∘})=$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272279 1072 #uuid "c98a2195-4ec8-5e2a-a4f5-76fb1079c255" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272279 1074 "First, we recall the difference formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nApplying the difference formula for cosine with $u=x$ and $v={150}^{∘},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-{150}^{∘}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡{150}^{∘}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡{150}^{∘} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272280 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271632 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272280 1069 "q-141375" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272280 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272280 1071 "$\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-{180}^{∘})=$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272280 1072 #uuid "a93f808f-0321-5136-8fe8-ad8e11f31b22" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272280 1074 "First, we recall the difference formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nApplying the difference formula for cosine with $u=x$ and $v={180}^{∘},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-{180}^{∘}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡{180}^{∘}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡{180}^{∘} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot (-1)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272281 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271629 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272281 1069 "q-141381" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272281 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272281 1071 "$\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+\\frac{3\\pi }{4})=$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272281 1072 #uuid "308956a9-f5f0-59d1-8748-67ba18adb1f8" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272281 1074 "First, we recall the sum formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nApplying the sum formula for cosine with $u=x$ and $v=\\frac{3\\pi }{4},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+\\frac{3\\pi }{4}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272282 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271706 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272282 1069 "q-141383" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272282 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272282 1071 "$\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+\\frac{5\\pi }{6})=$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272282 1072 #uuid "273cf5d2-8279-56b4-b179-bc318d5590ca" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272282 1074 "First, we recall the sum formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nApplying the sum formula for cosine with $u=x$ and $v=\\frac{5\\pi }{6},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+\\frac{5\\pi }{6}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{6})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272283 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271694 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272283 1069 "q-141386" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272283 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272283 1071 "$\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})=$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272283 1072 #uuid "bedf2d85-2b35-59e9-bfbf-70576d25817c" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272283 1074 "First, we recall  the difference formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nApplying the difference formula for cosine with $u=x$ and $v=\\frac{\\pi }{3},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272284 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271535 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272284 1069 "q-14141" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272284 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272284 1071 "For the polynomial $f(x)=4{x}^{5}-4{x}^{2}+16x-16,$ how many *potential* roots are given by the rational roots theorem?" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272284 1072 #uuid "52c32cb4-13fd-5370-99b6-582dfa832135" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272284 1074 "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial:\n\n- The constant term is $-16,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 2,\\,\\pm 4,\\,\\pm 8$ and $\\pm 16.$\n\n- The leading coefficient is $4,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 2$ and $\\pm 4.$\n\nFirst, we list out all the possibilities for the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm \\frac{1}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{4}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{2}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{4}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{4}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{4}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{4}{4}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{8}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{8}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{8}{4}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{16}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{16}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{16}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we reduce the fractions to lowest terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{4}, \\\\ \\pm 2, & \\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, \\\\ \\pm 4, & \\pm 2, & \\pm 1, \\\\ \\pm 8, & \\pm 4, & \\pm 2, \\\\ \\pm 16, & \\pm 8, & \\pm 4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that some potential roots are duplicates (shown above in red). Removing these duplicates, and listing those remaining in order from least to greatest in absolute value, we have\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{1}{4},\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 2,\\,\\pm 4,\\,\\pm 8,\\,\\pm 16.\n$$\n\nThis makes a total of $14$ potential roots. (Remember that each $\\pm $ sign accounts for $2$ roots, and $2\\cdot 7=14.$)" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272285 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271527 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272285 1069 "q-14142" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272285 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272285 1071 "For the polynomial $f(x)=6{x}^{4}-15{x}^{2}-2x+5,$ how many *potential* roots are given by the rational roots theorem?" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272285 1072 #uuid "b0caeff2-4a23-5ec8-b046-51ecdd035f70" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272285 1074 "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial:\n\n- The constant term is $5,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1$ and $\\pm 5.$\n\n- The leading coefficient is $6,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 2,\\,\\pm 3$ and $\\pm 6.$\n\nFirst, we list out all the possibilities for the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm \\frac{1}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{3}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{6}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{5}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{5}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{5}{3}, & \\pm \\frac{5}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we reduce the fractions to lowest terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{3}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{6}, \\\\ \\pm 5, & \\pm \\frac{5}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{5}{3}, & \\pm \\frac{5}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that none of these potential roots are duplicates. Listing those potential roots in order from least to greatest in absolute value, we have\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{1}{6},\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{3},\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{2},\\,\\pm \\frac{5}{6},\\,\\pm 1,\\,\\pm \\frac{5}{3},\\,\\pm \\frac{5}{2},\\,\\pm 5.\n$$\n\nThis makes a total of $16$ potential roots. (Remember that each $\\pm $ sign accounts for $2$ roots, and $2\\cdot 8=16.$)" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272286 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271857 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272286 1069 "q-14144" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272286 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272286 1071 "For the polynomial $f(x)=3{x}^{8}-18{x}^{2}-33x-18,$ how many *potential* roots are given by the rational roots theorem?" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272286 1072 #uuid "34dfcc70-f55e-5b7a-a24a-4dbf1116a030" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272286 1074 "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial:\n\n- The constant term is $-18,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 2,\\,\\pm 3,\\,\\pm 6,\\,\\pm 9$ and  $\\pm 18.$\n\n- The leading coefficient is $3,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1$ and $\\pm 3.$\n\nFirst, we list out all the possibilities for the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm \\frac{1}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{3}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{2}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{3}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{3}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{3}{3}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{6}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{6}{3}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{9}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{9}{3}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{18}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{18}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we reduce the fractions to lowest terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{1}{3}, \\\\ \\pm 2, & \\pm \\frac{2}{3}, \\\\ \\pm 3, & \\pm 1, \\\\ \\pm 6, & \\pm 2, \\\\ \\pm 9, & \\pm 3, \\\\ \\pm 18, & \\pm 6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that some potential roots are duplicates (shown above in red). Removing these duplicates, and listing those remaining in order from least to greatest in absolute value, we have\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{1}{3},\\,\\pm \\frac{2}{3}\\,\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 2,\\,\\pm 3,\\,\\pm 6,\\,\\pm 9,\\,\\pm 18.\n$$\n\nThis makes a total of $16$ potential roots. (Remember that each $\\pm $ sign accounts for $2$ roots, and $2\\cdot 8=16.$)" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272287 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271627 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272287 1069 "q-141444" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272287 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272287 1071 "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡u=\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{3},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡u=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡v=\\frac{4}{5}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $v$ lies in the second quadrant, and\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v)=\\frac{a\\sqrt{6}-b\\sqrt{3}}{15},\n$$\n\ncalculate the value of $a+b.$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272287 1072 #uuid "1c51674c-fd7a-5949-82f4-633efacaa379" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272287 1074 "First, we recall the difference formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡u$, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡u$, and $\\operatorname{sin}⁡v.$ To find the missing value of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,$ we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡v+{cos}^{2}⁡v=1\\,⟹\\,{cos}^{2}⁡v=1-{sin}^{2}⁡v\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡v & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡v \\\\  & =1-{(\\frac{4}{5})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{4}^{2}}{{5}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{16}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{25}-\\frac{16}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{9}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{3}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $v$ lies in the second quadrant, where cosine is negative. So, we disregard the positive value, and we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡v=-\\frac{3}{5}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the difference formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3})(-\\frac{3}{5})+(\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{3})(\\frac{4}{5}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{15}+\\frac{4\\sqrt{6}}{15} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\sqrt{6}-3\\sqrt{3}}{15}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=4,b=3,$ and $a+b=7.$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272288 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271779 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272288 1069 "q-141447" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272288 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272288 1071 "$T$ is a bijection $\\,⟺\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)= \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=n \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n$\\,⟺\\,$  $T$ is invertible\n\nConsider the equivalence above, where $T:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n}$ is a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T.$ Which of the following should be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272288 1072 #uuid "b9558da9-260d-5307-957b-694ef011704b" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272288 1074 "Recall that the *invertible matrix theorem* (in terms of linear transformations) states the following: \n\n $T$ is a bijection $\\,⟺\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{n} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=n \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n$\\,⟺\\,$  $T$ is invertible\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is ${R}^{n}.$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272289 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271542 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272289 1069 "q-141451" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272289 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272289 1071 "$T$ is a bijection $\\,⟺\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{n} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=n \\\\  & ={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n$\\,⟺\\,$  $T$ is invertible\n\nConsider the equivalence above, where $T:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n}$ is a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T.$ Which of the following should be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272289 1072 #uuid "74b33e25-b150-5023-89c8-8ae56ab3acbd" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272289 1074 "Recall that the *invertible matrix theorem* (in terms of linear transformations) states the following: \n\n $T$ is a bijection $\\,⟺\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{n} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=n \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n$\\,⟺\\,$  $T$ is invertible\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is $\\text{Ker}(T).$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272290 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271578 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272290 1069 "q-141454" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272290 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272290 1071 "$T$ is a bijection $\\,⟺\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & ={R}^{n} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=n \\\\  & ={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n$\\,⟺\\,$  $T$ is invertible\n\nConsider the equivalence above, where $T:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n}$ is a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T.$ **From top to bottom**, which of the following should be placed into the blank spaces to make the statements true?" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272290 1072 #uuid "55000931-5b53-577e-bfdc-cc3083a1d40b" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272290 1074 "Recall that the *invertible matrix theorem* (in terms of linear transformations) states the following: \n\n $T$ is a bijection $\\,⟺\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{n} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=n \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n$\\,⟺\\,$  $T$ is invertible\n\nTherefore, from top to bottom the missing expression are $\\text{Im}(T)$ and $\\text{Ker}(T).$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272291 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271595 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272291 1069 "q-141457" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272291 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272291 1071 "$T$ is a bijection $\\,⟺\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)= \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)= \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n$\\,⟺\\,$  $T$ is invertible\n\nConsider the equivalence above, where $T:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n}$ is a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T.$ **From top to bottom**, which of the following should be placed into the blank spaces to make the statements true?" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272291 1072 #uuid "d0c34404-67b0-5bd2-86a3-b5bd22c70fb5" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272291 1074 "Recall that the *invertible matrix theorem* (in terms of linear transformations) states the following: \n\n $T$ is a bijection $\\,⟺\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{n} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=n \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n$\\,⟺\\,$  $T$ is invertible\n\nTherefore, from top to bottom, the missing expressions are ${R}^{n}$ and $n.$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272292 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271571 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272292 1069 "q-14146" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272292 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272292 1071 "According to the rational roots theorem, which of the following could be a root of the polynomial $f(x)=2{x}^{3}-7{x}^{2}-5x+4?$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272292 1072 #uuid "1c724d68-7758-5ee9-9792-a8e5822b6f11" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272292 1074 "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $4,$ and the leading coefficient is $2.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }4}{\\text{factor of }2}.\n$$\n\nOf the given answer choices, only $\\frac{1}{2}$ takes the above form since the numerator $(1)$ is a factor of $4$ and the denominator $(2)$ is a factor of $2.$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272293 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271599 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272293 1069 "q-141460" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272293 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272293 1071 "Which of the following statements are true given that $T:{R}^{7}\\to {R}^{7}$ is a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T,$ and $T(x)=v$ has no solution for some vector $v?$ \n\n- $T$ is not invertible\n- $\\text{nullity}(T)=0$\n- $\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 7$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272293 1072 #uuid "c894da3b-5f0a-59a9-8060-52795d04ecee" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272293 1074 "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{7}$ into ${R}^{7},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{7} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=7 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Since $T(x)=v$ has no solution, we have that $v\\notin \\text{Im}(T),$ hence $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{7}.$ According to the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{7},$ the transformation $T$ and the matrix $T$ are not invertible.\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $T$ is not invertible, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{nullity}(T)\\ne 0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $T$ is not invertible, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 7.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272294 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271597 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272294 1069 "q-141461" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272294 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272294 1071 "Which of the following statements are true given that $T:{R}^{4}\\to {R}^{4}$ is a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T,$ and $T$ is a bijection? \n\n- $\\text{Ker}(T)={0}$\n- $\\text{rank}(T)=4$\n- $T(v)=0$ for some $v\\ne 0$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272294 1072 #uuid "2fc1f275-1b92-5d39-91a9-c77fc478abd0" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272294 1074 "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{4}$ into ${R}^{4},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{4} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=4 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Since $T$ is a bijection, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{Ker}(T)={0}.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $T$ is a bijection, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{rank}(T)=4.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $T$ is a bijection, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{Ker}(T)={0},$ which means there is no non-zero vector $v$ such that $T(v)=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272295 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271787 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272295 1069 "q-14148" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272295 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272295 1071 "According to the rational roots theorem, which of the following **cannot** be a root of the polynomial  $f(x)=2{x}^{3}+13{x}^{2}+10x-25?$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272295 1072 #uuid "708d6312-3909-5627-901e-1ca20b3fbaad" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272295 1074 "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $-25$ and the leading coefficient is $2.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }-25}{\\text{factor of }2}.\n$$\n\nOf the given answer choices, only $15$ does not take the above form. The value $15$ can be interpreted as a fraction $\\frac{15}{1},$ and the numerator $(15)$ is not a factor of $-25.$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272296 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271911 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272296 1069 "q-141481" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272296 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272296 1071 "![](images/5c/5c7723e43696fac78180663115c50ed76c81c8841e3a99eb26122860f798dcc4.png)\n\nConsider the diagram shown above, where $\\overset{\\to }{QT}=a,$ $\\overset{\\to }{RS}=b,$ $\\overset{\\to }{TR}=c,$ and $\\overset{\\to }{RP}=d.$ Find $\\overset{\\to }{QP}$ in terms of $a,b,c,$ and $d.$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272296 1072 #uuid "bf1d84f6-52a0-5519-83a9-c2524eb94159" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272296 1074 "We want to find a \"path\" from $Q$ to $P$ consisting only of the vectors $a,b,c,$ and $d.$\n\nAccording to the triangle law of addition,\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{QP}=\\overset{\\to }{QT}+\\overset{\\to }{TR}+\\overset{\\to }{RP}\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/da/da5a61cf57c3ffcf82b693b8738334bc840a13b5f34068858e20453906d1b6f2.png)\n\n  \n\nNow, from the original diagram, we have that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{QT}=a,\\,\\overset{\\to }{TR}=c,\\,\\overset{\\to }{RP}=d.\n$$\n\nLet's add these to our diagram.\n\n![](images/c3/c3cb5fabcbd664b0e5eef879cf54f7a5b5d0f187c2f3d68a71d3c91b972005e3.png)\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{QP} & =\\overset{\\to }{QT}+\\overset{\\to }{TR}+\\overset{\\to }{RP} \\\\  & =a+c+d.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272297 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271801 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272297 1069 "q-141495" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272297 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272297 1071 "Let $T:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{3}$ be a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T,$ and ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\in {R}^{3}.$  Consider the following statement:\n\n*If $2T({v}_{1})-3T({v}_{2})=0$ and ${v}_{1}\\ne k{v}_{2}$ for all $k\\in R$ then $\\text{__________}.$*\n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n\n- $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}$\n- $\\text{Im}(T)={R}^{3}$\n- $T$ is invertible" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272297 1072 #uuid "496f9381-a057-5d31-a929-726bf731d70b" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272297 1074 "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{3}$ into ${R}^{3},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=3 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Since $2T({v}_{1})-3T({v}_{2})=0$ and $T$ is a linear transformation, we have $T(2{v}_{1}-3{v}_{2})=0$ which means $2{v}_{1}-3{v}_{2}\\in \\text{Ker}(T).$   Since ${v}_{1}\\ne k{v}_{2}$,  we must have $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem, the transformation $T$ is not a bijection. Since $T$ is not a bijection, then by the invertible matrix theorem, $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{3}.$\n\n- Statement III is false. According to the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{3},$ the corresponding matrix $T$ can't be invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272298 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271933 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272298 1069 "q-141513" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272298 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272298 1071 "Rewrite the expression $({y}^{2}+12y+{6}^{2})-3$ as the difference between a perfect square and a constant." 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272298 1072 #uuid "48f5b61f-2f3f-5cda-a551-c1e861cdd220" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272298 1074 "First, we recall the following formula:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}+2ay+{a}^{2}=(y+a{)}^{2}\n$$\n\nNotice that we have a perfect square in the parentheses that we can factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}+12y+{6}^{2} & = \\\\ {y}^{2}+2\\cdot 6y+{6}^{2} & = \\\\ (y+6{)}^{2} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({y}^{2}+12y+{6}^{2})-3 & = \\\\ (y+6{)}^{2}-3 & .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272299 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271557 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272299 1069 "q-141522" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272299 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272299 1071 "Complete the square on the quadratic expression ${x}^{2}+x-2.$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272299 1072 #uuid "54091288-1b32-5047-b279-479983ae80b9" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272299 1074 "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}x-2\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2},$ writing the constant term $-2$ last:\n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}x+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}}_{⏟}-{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-2\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(x+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-2\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(x+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{9}{4}.\n$$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272300 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271634 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272300 1069 "q-141527" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272300 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272300 1071 "Complete the square on the quadratic expression ${y}^{2}+11y+25.$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272300 1072 #uuid "b17fd33d-fef8-5118-8c44-b94f68ba1d49" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272300 1074 "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{11}{2}y+25\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{11}{2})}^{2},$ writing the constant term $25$ last:\n\n$$\n{{y}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{11}{2}y+{(\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}}_{⏟}-{(\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}+25\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(y+\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}+25\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(y+\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{21}{4}.\n$$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272301 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271654 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272301 1069 "q-141536" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272301 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272301 1071 "Complete the square on the quadratic expression ${y}^{2}+7y.$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272301 1072 #uuid "f6ca9c1a-eab9-55fb-98fa-ee3277281e1b" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272301 1074 "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{7}{2}y\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}:$\n\n$$\n{{y}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{7}{2}y+{(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}}_{⏟}-{(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which 