 1072 #uuid "c4507db3-2027-5030-88fd-955381171b17" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272307 1074 "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{4}$ into ${R}^{4},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{4} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=4 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since $\\text{nullity}(T)\\ne 0,$ the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{4}.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, since $\\text{nullity}(T)\\ne 0,$ the invertible matrix theorem implies that $T$ is not invertible.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since $T$ is not invertible, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 4.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272308 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271607 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272308 1069 "q-141584" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272308 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272308 1071 "Let $T:{R}^{6}\\to {R}^{6}$ be a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T.$ Consider the following statement:\n\n*If ${v}_{1}\\ne {v}_{2}$ vectors such that $T({v}_{1})=T({v}_{2})$, then $\\text{__________}.$*\n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n\n- $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}$\n- $\\text{Im}(T)={R}^{6}$\n- $T$ is not a bijection" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272308 1072 #uuid "2805dfb0-8d9a-5775-8051-a005b74b03a8" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272308 1074 "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{6}$ into ${R}^{6},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{6} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=6 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$\n\nSince $T$ is a linear transformation, we have that\n\n$$\nT({v}_{1})=T({v}_{2})\\,⟹\\,T({v}_{1}-{v}_{2})=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Since ${v}_{1}-{v}_{2}\\in \\text{Ker}(T)$ and ${v}_{1}\\ne {v}_{2},$ we must have that $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}.$\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0},$ the transformation $T$ and the matrix $T$ are not invertible. Since $T$ is not invertible, by the invertible matrix theorem, we must have that $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{6}.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{6},$ the invertible matrix theorem implies that the transformation $T$ is not a bijection.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272309 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271726 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272309 1069 "q-141611" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272309 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272309 1071 "Complete the square on the quadratic expression ${x}^{2}-10x+19.$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272309 1072 #uuid "d31dbfbd-45d5-55ae-921a-1aba0a785ab0" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272309 1074 "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 5x+19\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${5}^{2},$ writing the constant term $19$ last:\n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 5x+{5}^{2}}_{⏟}-\\,{5}^{2}+19\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-5{)}^{2}-{5}^{2}+19\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(x-5{)}^{2}-6.\n$$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272310 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271524 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272310 1069 "q-141612" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272310 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272310 1071 "Complete the square on the quadratic expression ${y}^{2}-4y+10.$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272310 1072 #uuid "7c43e9bd-a43d-5cc8-ba55-c5e66008e58c" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272310 1074 "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-2\\cdot 2y+10\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${2}^{2},$ writing the constant term $10$ last:\n\n$$\n{{y}^{2}-2\\cdot 2y+{2}^{2}}_{⏟}-\\,{2}^{2}+10\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(y-2{)}^{2}-{2}^{2}+10\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(y-2{)}^{2}+6.\n$$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272311 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271708 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272311 1069 "q-141627" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272311 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272311 1071 "The equation $3{z}^{2}-15=12z$ can be written in the form $(z-p)^{2}=q.$ Find $q,$ and solve the equation." 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272311 1072 #uuid "948cfe82-2b59-583b-87a6-8211c3b5ce23" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272311 1074 "First, we move the constant term to the right-hand side, divide it by the coefficient of the quadratic term, and complete the square on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{z}^{2}-15 & =12z \\\\ 3{z}^{2}-12z & =15 \\\\ 3({z}^{2}-4z) & =15 \\\\ {z}^{2}-4z & =5 \\\\ {z}^{2}-2\\cdot 2\\cdot z & =5 \\\\ {z}^{2}-2\\cdot 2\\cdot z+{2}^{2}-{2}^{2} & =5 \\\\ {(z-2)}^{2}-{2}^{2} & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we isolate the perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(z-2)}^{2}-{2}^{2} & =5 \\\\ {(z-2)}^{2}-4 & =5 \\\\ {(z-2)}^{2} & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $p=2$ and $q=9.$\n\nNext, we solve the equation using the square-root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{(z-2)}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{9} \\\\ |z-2| & =3 \\\\ z-2 & =\\pm 3 \\\\ z & =2\\pm 3 \\\\ z & =-1,\\,5\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272312 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271953 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272312 1069 "q-141751" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272312 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272312 1071 "![](images/9d/9d5ea7e13fa03ac553893aa78aa7ee32ac7b67f2eb19dff32228d53bf2010aa2.png)\n\nWhich of the curves above are concave down?" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272312 1072 #uuid "1bb3b4f0-a153-5b1f-93aa-f136a6132e95" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272312 1074 "The concavity of a function refers to the direction in which its graph curves.\n\n- If the graph curves upward, like a \"smile\" ($⌣$), then the graph is said to be *concave up*.\n\n- If the graph curves downward, like a \"frown\" ($⌢$), then the graph is said to be *concave down*.\n\nIt's helpful to remember the mnemonic \"concave down like a frown.\"\n\nOnly graphs I and III curve downward (like a \"frown\") among the given graphs." 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272313 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271691 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272313 1069 "q-141754" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272313 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272313 1071 "![](images/b2/b2b904027ec6319fe5857f81089469568322d148e49cdf31bb20c07f1f31daf4.png)\n\nWhich of the curves above are concave down?" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272313 1072 #uuid "13227862-4183-5d5b-b484-99ef3e29fd6b" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272313 1074 "The concavity of a function refers to the direction in which its graph curves.\n\n- If the graph curves upward, like a \"smile\" ($⌣$), then the graph is said to be *concave up*.\n\n- If the graph curves downward, like a \"frown\" ($⌢$), then the graph is said to be *concave down*.\n\nIt's helpful to remember the mnemonic \"concave down like a frown.\"\n\nAll graphs I, II, and III curve downward (like a \"frown\") among the given graphs." 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272314 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271817 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272314 1069 "q-141804" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272314 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272314 1071 "Let ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ be the solutions of the equation ${x}^{2}+4x=12.$ Find the value ${x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}.$ \n\n*Hint: Use the fact that ${x}^{2}+4x+4$ is a perfect square.*" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272314 1072 #uuid "1b9295ce-b641-57bd-b519-585112fb257f" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272314 1074 "Using the hint, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+4x+4=(x+2{)}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we complete the square on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+4x & =12 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2\\cdot 2\\cdot x & =12 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2\\cdot 2\\cdot x+{2}^{2}-{2}^{2} & =12 \\\\ (x+2{)}^{2}-4 & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we isolate the perfect square by adding $4$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+2{)}^{2}-4+4 & =12+4 \\\\ (x+2{)}^{2} & =16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+2{)}^{2} & =16 \\\\ \\sqrt{(x+2{)}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{16} \\\\ |x+2| & =4 \\\\ x+2 & =\\pm 4 \\\\ x & =-2\\pm 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify the two solutions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =-2-4=-6 \\\\ {x}_{2} & =-2+4=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}=(-6{)}^{2}+{2}^{2}=40.\n$$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272315 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271553 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272315 1069 "q-141812" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272315 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272315 1071 "Let ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ be the solutions of the equation ${x}^{2}-8x=20.$ Find the value ${x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}.$ \n\n*Hint: Use the fact that ${x}^{2}-8x+16$ is a perfect square.*" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272315 1072 #uuid "f0de10d9-8ebd-58e0-b040-9c2f5e59c9c7" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272315 1074 "Using the hint, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-8x+16=(x-4{)}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we complete the square on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-8x & =20 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2\\cdot 4\\cdot x & =20 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2\\cdot 4\\cdot x+{4}^{2}-{4}^{2} & =20 \\\\ (x-4{)}^{2}-16 & =20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we isolate the perfect square by adding $16$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-4{)}^{2}-16+16 & =20+16 \\\\ (x-4{)}^{2} & =36\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-4{)}^{2} & =36 \\\\ \\sqrt{(x-4{)}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{36} \\\\ |x-4| & =6 \\\\ x-4 & =\\pm 6 \\\\ x & =4\\pm 6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify the two solutions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =4-6=-2 \\\\ {x}_{2} & =4+6=10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}=(-2{)}^{2}+{10}^{2}=104.\n$$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272316 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271950 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272316 1069 "q-141817" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272316 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272316 1071 "Solve the equation $5{y}^{2}-40y=-76.$ \n\n*Hint: Use the fact that ${y}^{2}-8y+16$ is a perfect square.*" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272316 1072 #uuid "7c3581b3-19ec-58f3-879c-94addceac5e6" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272316 1074 "Using the hint, we have\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-8y+16=(y-4)^{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we complete the square on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{y}^{2}-40y & =-76 \\\\ 5({y}^{2}-8y) & =-76 \\\\ 5({y}^{2}-2\\cdot 4y) & =-76 \\\\ 5({y}^{2}-2\\cdot 4y+{4}^{2}-{4}^{2}) & =-76 \\\\ 5[(y-4)^{2}-{4}^{2}] & =-76 \\\\ 5[(y-4)^{2}-16] & =-76 \\\\ 5(y-4)^{2}-80 & =-76\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIsolating the perfect square by adding $80$ to both sides and then dividing by $5,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(y-4)^{2}-80 & =-76 \\\\ 5(y-4)^{2}-80+80 & =-76+80 \\\\ 5(y-4)^{2} & =4 \\\\ (y-4)^{2} & =\\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-4)^{2} & =\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ \\sqrt{(y-4)^{2}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{4}{5}} \\\\ |y-4| & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ y-4 & =\\pm \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ y & =4\\pm \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272317 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271937 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272317 1069 "q-141824" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272317 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272317 1071 "Solve the equation $-6{y}^{2}+24y=21.$ \n\n*Hint: Use the fact that ${y}^{2}-4y+4$ is a perfect square.*" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272317 1072 #uuid "03b9f129-6f00-5dcf-9f10-c4784f08b856" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272317 1074 "Using the hint, we have\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-4y+4=(y-2)^{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we complete the square on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6{y}^{2}+24y & =21 \\\\ -6({y}^{2}-4y) & =21 \\\\ -6({y}^{2}-2\\cdot 2y) & =21 \\\\ -6({y}^{2}-2\\cdot 2y+{2}^{2}-{2}^{2}) & =21 \\\\ -6[(y-2)^{2}-{2}^{2}] & =21 \\\\ -6[(y-2)^{2}-4] & =21 \\\\ -6(y-2)^{2}+(-6)(-4) & =21 \\\\ -6(y-2)^{2}+24 & =21\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIsolating the perfect square by subtracting $24$ from both sides and then dividing by $(-6),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6(y-2)^{2}+24 & =21 \\\\ -6(y-2)^{2}+24-24 & =21-24 \\\\ -6(y-2)^{2} & =-3 \\\\ (y-2)^{2} & =\\frac{(-3)}{(-6)} \\\\ (y-2)^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-2)^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\sqrt{(y-2)^{2}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{2}} \\\\ |y-2| & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ y-2 & =\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ y & =2\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272318 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271641 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272318 1069 "q-141826" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272318 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272318 1071 "Solve the equation $-7{y}^{2}-42y=62.$ \n\n*Hint: Use the fact that ${y}^{2}+6y+9$ is a perfect square.*" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272318 1072 #uuid "a2ec4003-7bb2-5f7b-b2d0-ca23e28bb9c5" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272318 1074 "Using the hint, we have\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}+6y+9=(y+3)^{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we complete the square on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-7{y}^{2}-42y & =62 \\\\ -7({y}^{2}+6y) & =62 \\\\ -7({y}^{2}+2\\cdot 3y) & =62 \\\\ -7({y}^{2}+2\\cdot 3y+{3}^{2}-{3}^{2}) & =62 \\\\ -7[(y+3)^{2}-{3}^{2}] & =62 \\\\ -7[(y+3)^{2}-9] & =62 \\\\ -7(y+3)^{2}+(-7)(-9) & =62 \\\\ -7(y+3)^{2}+63 & =62\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIsolating the perfect square by subtracting $63$ from both sides and then dividing by $(-7),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-7(y+3)^{2}+63 & =62 \\\\ -7(y+3)^{2}+63-63 & =62-63 \\\\ -7(y+3)^{2} & =-1 \\\\ (y+3)^{2} & =\\frac{(-1)}{(-7)} \\\\ (y+3)^{2} & =\\frac{1}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+3)^{2} & =\\frac{1}{7} \\\\ \\sqrt{(y+3)^{2}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{7}} \\\\ |y+3| & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{7}} \\\\ y+3 & =\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{7}} \\\\ y & =-3\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{7}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272319 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271518 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272319 1069 "q-141829" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272319 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272319 1071 "Solve the equation $-4{x}^{2}+4x=-2.$ \n\n*Hint: Use the fact that ${x}^{2}-x+\\frac{1}{4}$ is a perfect square.*" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272319 1072 #uuid "1044f32f-7390-525d-94d0-30c2ecd3e1b6" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272319 1074 "Using the hint, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-x+\\frac{1}{4}={(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we complete the square on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4{x}^{2}+4x & =-2 \\\\ -4({x}^{2}-x) & =-2 \\\\ -4({x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}x) & =-2 \\\\ -4({x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}x+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}) & =-2 \\\\ -4[{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}] & =-2 \\\\ -4[{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}] & =-2 \\\\ -4{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+(-4)(-\\frac{1}{4}) & =-2 \\\\ -4{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+1 & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIsolating the perfect square by subtracting $1$ from both sides and then dividing by $(-4),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+1 & =-2 \\\\ -4{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+1-1 & =-2-1 \\\\ -4{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =-3 \\\\ {(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =\\frac{(-3)}{(-4)} \\\\ {(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ \\sqrt{{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{4}} \\\\ |x-\\frac{1}{2}| & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ x-\\frac{1}{2} & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ x & =\\frac{1\\pm \\sqrt{3}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272320 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271738 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272320 1069 "q-14185" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272320 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272320 1071 "Find the point of intersection of the parabola ${y}^{2}-x+10y+21=0$ with the $x$-axis." 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272320 1072 #uuid "08773f73-4575-52a7-9948-7d0d1e95d791" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272320 1074 "When the parabola intersects the $x$-axis, we must have $y=0.$ So, we set $y=0$ and solve the resulting equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-x+10y+21 & =0 \\\\ (0)^{2}-x+10(0)+21 & =0 \\\\ -x+21 & =0 \\\\ x & =21\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parabola intersects the $x$-axis at $(21,0).$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272321 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271889 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272321 1069 "q-141856" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272321 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272321 1071 "![](images/3c/3cddc2df4c121c67442b8b2f838546177e47d90878f45906763b88f456235a8a.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=f(x),$ defined on $[-4,4].$ Find the intervals on which the function $f(x)$ is concave up." 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272321 1072 #uuid "b89d6797-0c33-56b8-bf65-5f0b98190f41" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272321 1074 "The concavity of a function refers to the direction in which its graph curves.\n\n- If the graph curves upward, like a \"smile\" ($⌣$), then the graph is said to be *concave up*.\n\n- If the graph curves downward, like a \"frown\" ($⌢$), then the graph is said to be *concave down*.\n\nIt's helpful to remember the mnemonic \"concave down like a frown.\"\n\nWe can see that the function is curved upward like a smile ($⌣$) over the interval $(-1,1).$\n\n![](images/b9/b962ef8adde197bca30dfc5d0efe5bcbc24fef8c1fad374992e0c571ef7cd320.png)" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272322 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271954 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272322 1069 "q-141859" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272322 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272322 1071 "![](images/f9/f97651190b02f5e85baec69d8915840f76c0179689f7fbc05d70a91027325d88.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=f(x),$ defined on $[0,3].$ Find the intervals on which the function $f(x)$ is concave down." 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272322 1072 #uuid "1d7ad854-ef7c-5f06-8f76-b7264b3fb245" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272322 1074 "The concavity of a function refers to the direction in which its graph curves.\n\n- If the graph curves upward, like a \"smile\" ($⌣$), then the graph is said to be *concave up*.\n\n- If the graph curves downward, like a \"frown\" ($⌢$), then the graph is said to be *concave down*.\n\nIt's helpful to remember the mnemonic \"concave down like a frown.\"\n\nWe can see that the function is curved downward like a frown ($⌢$) over the interval $(1,2).$\n\n![](images/2c/2ca2ab18c5a4945b197cfbf4e77e300ec73abed71ae7590063543742cbbf1322.png)" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272323 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271582 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272323 1069 "q-14190" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272323 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272323 1071 "Find the point of intersection of the parabola ${y}^{2}+18x-27y+54=0$ with the $x$-axis." 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272323 1072 #uuid "1c0ef7f3-cc1c-5bcc-a705-132d395570a5" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272323 1074 "When the parabola intersects the $x$-axis, we must have $y=0.$ So, we set $y=0$ and solve the resulting equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0)^{2}+18x-27(0)+54 & =0 \\\\ 18x+54 & =0 \\\\ 18x & =-54 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{54}{18} \\\\ x & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parabola intersects the $x$-axis at $(-3,0).$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272324 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271529 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272324 1069 "q-141937" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272324 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272324 1071 "![](images/58/583cde62650a6940389d807eee0ffeabdafcbb0da7b8d416af88e3c4d1b7bd71.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=f(x),$ defined on $[-3,0].$ Find the intervals on which the function $f(x)$ is concave down." 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272324 1072 #uuid "81b50d75-05a1-5fec-ae6a-c17be8793e34" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272324 1074 "The concavity of a function refers to the direction in which its graph curves.\n\n- If the graph curves upward, like a \"smile\" ($⌣$), then the graph is said to be *concave up*.\n\n- If the graph curves downward, like a \"frown\" ($⌢$), then the graph is said to be *concave down*.\n\nIt's helpful to remember the mnemonic \"concave down like a frown.\"\n\nWe can see that the function is curved downwards like a frown ($⌢$) over the intervals $(-2,-1)$ and $(-1,0)$\n\n![](images/ce/ce57fc818eebd0cc5007e80a54b49d613eeb2823eefc98e946862ff9f7b68e0b.png)" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272325 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271772 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272325 1069 "q-141974" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272325 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272325 1071 "![](images/2b/2b8bbc9ae6b8bf8c441c8e2b534fb159f8180833e1891baa094f988d6a362117.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(Bx)+D,$ where $B$ and $D$ are constants. What is $D-B?$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272325 1072 #uuid "d8e5a761-571d-5609-89c6-2c78742817c7" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272325 1074 "The period $T$ of the function is\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}-(-\\frac{3\\pi }{4})=\\frac{6\\pi }{4}=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nThe formula for the period $T$ of the function $y=A\\operatorname{tan}⁡(Bx+C)+D$ is given by \n\n$$\nT=\\frac{\\pi }{B}.\n$$\n\nApplying the formula of the period, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{3\\pi }{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{B}\\,⟹\\,B=\\frac{\\pi }{(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})}=\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nThe parameter $D$ represents the vertical shift of the tangent function. We see that the function has been translated by $1$ units up. Hence, $D=1.$\n\nTherefore, $D-B=1-(\\frac{2}{3})=\\frac{1}{3}.$\n\nFinally, the equation of the curve is\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{2x}{3})+1.\n$$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272326 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271696 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272326 1069 "q-142011" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272326 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272326 1071 "Let ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ be the solutions of the equation ${x}^{2}-6x=16.$ Find the value ${x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}.$ \n\n*Hint: Use the fact that ${x}^{2}-6x+9$ is a perfect square.*" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272326 1072 #uuid "b9162ac7-236e-5acd-bcca-ee24c2b8f68f" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272326 1074 "Using the hint, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-6x+9=(x-3{)}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we complete the square on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-6x & =16 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2\\cdot 3\\cdot x & =16 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2\\cdot 3\\cdot x+{3}^{2}-{3}^{2} & =16 \\\\ (x-3{)}^{2}-9 & =16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we isolate the perfect square by adding $9$ to both sides: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-3{)}^{2}-9+9 & =16+9 \\\\ (x-3{)}^{2} & =25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-3{)}^{2} & =25 \\\\ \\sqrt{(x-3{)}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{25} \\\\ |x-3| & =5 \\\\ x-3 & =\\pm 5 \\\\ x & =3\\pm 5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify the two solutions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =3-5=-2 \\\\ {x}_{2} & =3+5=8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}=(-2{)}^{2}+{8}^{2}=68.\n$$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272327 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271631 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272327 1069 "q-142015" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272327 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272327 1071 "Let ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ be the solutions of the equation ${x}^{2}-10x=-21.$ Find the value ${x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}.$ \n\n*Hint: Use the fact that ${x}^{2}-10x+25$ is a perfect square.*" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272327 1072 #uuid "36afe386-dcce-5814-bb16-64bd8f39ae6b" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272327 1074 "Using the hint, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-10x+25=(x-5{)}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we complete the square on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-10x & =-21 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2\\cdot 5\\cdot x & =-21 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2\\cdot 5\\cdot x+{5}^{2}-{5}^{2} & =-21 \\\\ (x-5{)}^{2}-25 & =-21\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we isolate the perfect square by adding $25$ to both sides: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-5{)}^{2}-25+25 & =-21+25 \\\\ (x-5{)}^{2} & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-5{)}^{2} & =4 \\\\ \\sqrt{(x-5{)}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\ |x-5| & =2 \\\\ x-5 & =\\pm 2 \\\\ x & =5\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify the two solutions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =5-2=3 \\\\ {x}_{2} & =5+2=7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}={3}^{2}+{7}^{2}=58.\n$$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272328 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271653 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272328 1069 "q-142025" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272328 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272328 1071 "$\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+\\frac{4\\pi }{3})=$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272328 1072 #uuid "85585a6b-0c0e-5963-819c-40902d9e809c" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272328 1074 "Recall the sum formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nApplying the sum formula for sine with $u=x$ and $v=\\frac{4\\pi }{3},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2}-\\sqrt{3}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272329 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271884 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272329 1069 "q-142026" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272329 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272329 1071 "$\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+\\frac{7\\pi }{6})=$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272329 1072 #uuid "81ad9fc7-9d0e-5199-bbb4-6a03531ef9d2" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272329 1074 "Recall the sum formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nApplying the sum formula for sine with $u=x$ and $v=\\frac{7\\pi }{6},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+\\frac{7\\pi }{6}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{6})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot (\\frac{-\\sqrt{3}}{2})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot (\\frac{-1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272330 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271789 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272330 1069 "q-142031" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272330 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272330 1071 "![](images/47/47b3d021487b88f0a335fc070bfd4846a0ea54dd137f6a0db165db5b27180d45.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. For which of the following values of $x$ is $f(x)$ concave up *and* ${f}^{′}(x)=0?$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272330 1072 #uuid "0bb121bf-9e48-5785-a2df-744a4832e310" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272330 1074 "The derivative ${f}^{′}(x)$ is zero when $x$ is a stationary point (the tangent line is horizontal).\n\nFrom the given graph, we see that only $x=c$ and $x=e$ are stationary points.\n\n![](images/89/899cb5979c943b1d385f9015ffa57bf97d4a47b10fb2be4e653272ed16494d73.png)\n\nThe concavity of a function refers to the direction in which its graph curves.\n\n- If the graph curves upward, like a \"smile\" ($⌣$), then the graph is said to be *concave up*.\n\n- If the graph curves downward, like a \"frown\" ($⌢$), then the graph is said to be *concave down*.\n\nIt's helpful to remember the mnemonic \"concave down like a frown.\"\n\nOf the points $x=c$ and $x=e,$ the graph is concave up ($⌣$) at $x=c$ only." 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272331 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271624 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272331 1069 "q-142065" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272331 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272331 1071 "![](images/de/de53aa4414f0c535562e0464b773928e505a98bea01afe5a12b2ec9b154b0b59.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. For which of the following values of $x$ is $f(x)$ concave up *and* ${f}^{′}(x)=0?$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272331 1072 #uuid "3b74a95b-dcae-5f21-b3cd-26c58658f85c" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272331 1074 "The derivative ${f}^{′}(x)$ is zero when $x$ is a stationary point (the tangent line is horizontal).\n\nFrom the given graph, we see that only $x=b$ and $x=d$ are stationary points.\n\n![](images/c7/c7a00e3b25a9fc1a9a589234d0beb421330b58cfe69d3e745f432c3f9be09485.png)\n\nThe concavity of a function refers to the direction in which its graph curves.\n\n- If the graph curves upward, like a \"smile\" ($⌣$), then the graph is said to be *concave up*.\n\n- If the graph curves downward, like a \"frown\" ($⌢$), then the graph is said to be *concave down*.\n\nIt's helpful to remember the mnemonic \"concave down like a frown.\"\n\nOf the points $x=b$ and $x=d,$ the graph is concave up ($⌣$) at $x=d$ only." 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272332 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271866 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272332 1069 "q-142068" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272332 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272332 1071 "![](images/d8/d8d2a012db4de4a2d9a008bae9d7dae854eb6071e0c5da6d1e65eea6b93f8c97.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. For which of the following values of $x$ is $f(x)$ concave up *and* ${f}^{′}(x)>0?$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272332 1072 #uuid "3befef56-fe55-5d23-9831-b2a33a1cd720" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272332 1074 "The derivative ${f}^{′}(x)$ is positive when $f(x)$ is increasing. From the given graph, we see that only $x=b$ and $x=c$ are on increasing parts of the graph.\n\n![](images/be/be95fe97afc8c90a372176e3dcf19fe49493d0e92059e7ceb5a4a40e77f23ee6.png)\n\nThe concavity of a function refers to the direction in which its graph curves.\n\n- If the graph curves upward, like a \"smile\" ($⌣$), then the graph is said to be *concave up*.\n\n- If the graph curves downward, like a \"frown\" ($⌢$), then the graph is said to be *concave down*.\n\nIt's helpful to remember the mnemonic \"concave down like a frown.\"\n\nOf the points $x=b$ and $x=c,$ the graph is concave up ($⌣$) at $x=b$ only." 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272333 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271835 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272333 1069 "q-142069" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272333 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272333 1071 "![](images/8d/8dbc36f0b404c1704e9add4b668b257703d6d163f825695b9404a33633da3ea7.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. For which of the following values of $x$ is $f(x)$ concave down *and* ${f}^{′}(x)>0?$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272333 1072 #uuid "a51e757c-6a44-5439-956b-057bca4d2d78" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272333 1074 "The derivative ${f}^{′}(x)$ is positive when $f(x)$ is increasing. From the given graph, we see that only $x=a$ and $x=e$ are on increasing parts of the graph.\n\n![](images/7f/7f42ed4bdb45d14497c70b0e2e98af95442d9bf8e6aaa577766dec1c5df2b31f.png)\n\nThe concavity of a function refers to the direction in which its graph curves.\n\n- If the graph curves upward, like a \"smile\" ($⌣$), then the graph is said to be *concave up*.\n\n- If the graph curves downward, like a \"frown\" ($⌢$), then the graph is said to be *concave down*.\n\nIt's helpful to remember the mnemonic \"concave down like a frown.\"\n\nOf the points $x=a$ and $x=e,$ the graph is concave  down ($⌢$) at $x=a$ only." 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272334 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271672 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272334 1069 "q-142070" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272334 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272334 1071 "![](images/84/84e5e0eb9f97521c7c9fb57c79b5bda67a1747fd0e608f7b466f20acc78ca308.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. For which of the following values of $x$ is $f(x)$ concave up *and* ${f}^{′}(x)=0?$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272334 1072 #uuid "7d2c7bcb-02cf-5190-bb03-b6fb648a8f3f" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272334 1074 "The derivative ${f}^{′}(x)$ is zero when $x$ is a stationary point (the tangent line is horizontal).\n\nFrom the given graph, we see that only $x=b$, $x=c$ and $x=d$ are stationary points.\n\n![](images/d5/d5e3e8e54b3c3c7f055f82433c6f8a68ffbc4590230fb827a0b8713fdd808a40.png)\n\nThe concavity of a function refers to the direction in which its graph curves.\n\n- If the graph curves upward, like a \"smile\" ($⌣$), then the graph is said to be *concave up*.\n\n- If the graph curves downward, like a \"frown\" ($⌢$), then the graph is said to be *concave down*.\n\nIt's helpful to remember the mnemonic \"concave down like a frown.\"\n\nOf the points $x=b$, $x=c$ and $x=d,$ the graph is concave up ($⌣$) at $x=c$ only." 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272335 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271519 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272335 1069 "q-142071" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272335 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272335 1071 "Rewrite $-({y}^{2}-11y+30)$ by completing the square." 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272335 1072 #uuid "801928f5-f859-5bec-ad10-ef9e1c04ab4f" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272335 1074 "We begin by completing the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-11y+30\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{11}{2}y+30\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{11}{2})}^{2},$ writing the constant term $30$ last:\n\n$$\n{{y}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{11}{2}y+{(\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}}_{⏟}-{(\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}+30\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(y-\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}+30\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(y-\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-({y}^{2}-11y+30) & =-({(y-\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =-{(y-\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}-{(y-\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272336 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271720 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272336 1069 "q-142113" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272336 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272336 1071 "Which of the following shaded areas describes the region bounded by the inequality $2x+4y\\ge 8?$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272336 1072 #uuid "1971b997-81a0-56e3-8842-ec9be2800f62" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272336 1074 "First, we convert the inequality into slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+4y & \\ge 8 \\\\ 4y & \\ge -2x+8 \\\\ y & \\ge -\\frac{1}{2}x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed as usual. We start by drawing the corresponding straight line \n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{1}{2}x+2.\n$$\n\n Since the inequality involves a 'greater than *or equal to*' sign, the line $y=-\\frac{1}{2}x+2$ itself is part of the solution. Therefore, we draw a **solid line**.\n\nThen, we need to decide whether to shade above or below the line. The inequality \n\n$$\ny\\ge -\\frac{1}{2}x+2\n$$\n\n refers to the $y$-values that are *greater than* or equal to those on the line $y=-\\frac{1}{2}x+2,$ so we shade *above* the line.\n\n![](images/22/226415c7de8240eaa2a87a10e87d95c6dbf45df6263c4b36ea819cfec097a8a0.png)" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272337 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271821 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272337 1069 "q-142142" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272337 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272337 1071 "$\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-{210}^{∘})=$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272337 1072 #uuid "a1de0aea-4bea-5c0c-94bb-b2903c951763" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272337 1074 "First, we recall the difference formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nApplying the difference formula for cosine with $u=x$ and $v={210}^{∘},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-{210}^{∘}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡{210}^{∘}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡{210}^{∘} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272338 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271784 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272338 1069 "q-142177" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272338 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272338 1071 "Find the solution of $-5{x}^{2}>10x.$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272338 1072 #uuid "0e8d0b92-ba8d-5bb3-9b78-2293ca7eab2e" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272338 1074 "To solve this inequality using the graphical method, we first need to move all of the numbers to the left-hand side so that the right-hand side is $0.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5{x}^{2} & >10x \\\\ -5{x}^{2}-10x & >0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we want to find the values of $x$ for which the parabola $y=-5{x}^{2}-10x$ lies above the $x$-axis. Let's find the roots of the parabola by factoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5{x}^{2}-10x & =0 \\\\ -5x(x+2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that the roots are $x=0$ and $x=-2,$ and we can graph the parabola as follows:\n\n![](images/6c/6cb044d4c6280d77dd525f6e2a8702d9fcba1553c2d33411407d69e09a6b715b.png)\n\nSince we are interested in solving \n\n$$\n-5{x}^{2}-10x>0,\n$$\n\nwe look for the part of the parabola that is above the $x$-axis. \n\nBased on the graph, the solution is $-2<x<0.$ \n\nUsing interval notation, we write $x\\in (-2,0).$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272339 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271846 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272339 1069 "q-142180" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272339 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272339 1071 "Find the solution of $-{x}^{2}+9x-18\\ge 0.$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272339 1072 #uuid "503c0efb-79c2-51f6-ad5d-0c4262a2faa4" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272339 1074 "To solve the inequality \n\n$$\n-{x}^{2}+9x-18\\ge 0,\n$$\n\nwe need to find the values of $x$ for which the parabola \n\n$$\ny=-{x}^{2}+9x-18\n$$\n\nlies *above* the $x$-axis or touches this axis.\n\nFirst, let's find the roots of the parabola:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}+9x-18 & =0 \\\\ -({x}^{2}-9x+18) & =0 \\\\ -(x-3)(x-6) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the roots are $x=3$ and $x=6,$ and we can graph the parabola as follows:\n\n![](images/d4/d4c28d26425c3c5bfc95dece73c8d8cb74f395f2de04a2c2ee1b1fd69f8433ed.png)\n\nThe values marked in blue show all $x$-values where the parabola lies *above* the axis or touches the $x$-axis. \n\nSo, the solution to the inequality is $3\\le x\\le 6.$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272340 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271517 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272340 1069 "q-142284" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272340 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272340 1071 "![](images/b1/b162564c1a2ba7f963986afb6ccbfc187769c9d04204e51dbed6afe9bcee976c.png)\n\nConsider the diagram shown above, where $\\overset{\\to }{SP}=a,$ $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}=b,$ and $\\overset{\\to }{PT}=c.$ Find $\\overset{\\to }{SQ}$ in terms of $a,b$ and $c.$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272340 1072 #uuid "eceb051e-ce80-5ee0-bacf-52f1e91559a4" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272340 1074 "We want to find a \"path\" from $P$ to $Q$ consisting only of the vectors $a,b,$ and $c.$\n\nAccording to the triangle law of addition,\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{SQ}=\\overset{\\to }{SP}+\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/82/821f008f5c77a2b632794077eca4da1405fe9dd77d1dd000176c5fba99a8fd98.png)\n\nNow, from the original diagram, we have that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{SP}=a,\\,\\overset{\\to }{PQ}=b.\n$$\n\nLet's add these to our diagram.\n\n \n\n![](images/4f/4fb266a59e1d2a8ec28c4f304d05b19aa678346e429f91669590f47e9c15c9da.png)\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{SQ} & =\\overset{\\to }{SP}+\\overset{\\to }{PQ} \\\\  & =a+b\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272341 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271556 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272341 1069 "q-142302" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272341 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272341 1071 "$$\nA\\text{ is }\\text{ }\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{n} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=n=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the equivalence above, where $A$ is an $n\\,\\times \\,n$ matrix. Which of the following should be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272341 1072 #uuid "bc47f988-d3cd-5d52-b9cf-e6a5355571de" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272341 1074 "Recall that the *invertible matrix theorem* states the following: \n\n*Let $A$ be an $n\\,\\times \\,n$ matrix. Then we have the following equivalence:*\n\n$$\nA\\text{ is }\\text{invertible}\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{n} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=n=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is \"invertible\"." 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272342 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271551 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272342 1069 "q-142314" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272342 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272342 1071 "Find the solution of ${x}^{2}+x-12\\le 0.$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272342 1072 #uuid "516a42b4-18d7-5e17-814b-bcd4a9e53678" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272342 1074 "To solve the inequality \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+x-12\\le 0,\n$$\n\nwe need to find the values of $x$ for which the parabola \n\n$$\ny={x}^{2}+x-12\n$$\n\nlies *below* the $x$-axis or touches this axis.\n\nFirst, let's find the roots of the parabola:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+x-12 & =0 \\\\ (x+4)(x-3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the roots are $x=-4$ and $x=3,$ and we can graph the parabola as follows:\n\n![](images/ed/eda4704129b905a10e379254dfd6d1ff07d593d7f97bbc6126e9d27bfec2f9f2.png)\n\nThe values marked in blue show all $x$-values where the parabola lies *below* the $x$-axis or touches this axis.\n\nSo, the solution to the inequality is $-4\\le x\\le 3.$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272343 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271614 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272343 1069 "q-142357" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272343 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272343 1071 "$$\nA\\text{ is invertible}\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & ={R}^{n} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=n=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the equivalence above, where $A$ is an $n\\,\\times \\,n$ matrix. Which of the following should be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272343 1072 #uuid "5d5626a2-9938-5097-90ed-3e9019e50489" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272343 1074 "Recall that the *invertible matrix theorem* states the following: \n\n*Let $A$ be an $n\\,\\times \\,n$ matrix. Then we have the following equivalence:*\n\n$$\nA\\text{ is invertible}\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{n} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=n=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is $\\text{Col}(A).$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272344 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271845 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272344 1069 "q-142358" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272344 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272344 1071 "Which of the following statements are true, given that $A$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix and \n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(A)={[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{1}\\end{aligned}]\\,|\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in R}?\n$$\n\n- $\\text{dim(Null}(A))=0$\n- $A$ is  invertible\n- $\\text{Col}(A)\\ne {R}^{5}$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272344 1072 #uuid "0c320bbb-0961-56b0-99e4-beaa75aa8f10" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272344 1074 "Since $A$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{5} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=5=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since \n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(A)={{x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,|\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in R}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that \n\n$$\n\\text{dim(Null}(A))=2\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $\\text{dim(Null}(A))\\ne 0,$ then according to the invertible matrix theorem, the matrix $A$ can't be invertible.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $A$ is not invertible, then by the invertible matrix theorem, we must have $\\text{Col}(A)\\ne {R}^{5}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272345 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271847 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272345 1069 "q-142369" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272345 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272345 1071 "![](images/40/40375a9794d9e399327cfcf128e017f05d20319fd86d41a8a9f49e01f58ccae0.png)\n\nFind $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for the polar curve $r=4$  (shown above) at $\\theta =\\frac{7\\pi }{4}.$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272345 1072 #uuid "e4c02ea3-6977-53a9-ae04-f813c6f1d08f" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272345 1074 "To find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use the formula for differentiating parametric curves:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}\n$$\n\nFirst, we recall that to convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $r=4,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating the necessary derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\theta ) & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\ =-\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we arrive at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{\\theta =7\\pi /4} & =-\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =-(-1) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272346 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271658 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272346 1069 "q-142382" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272346 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272346 1071 "Find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for the polar curve $r=12$ at $\\theta =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272346 1072 #uuid "4ae6bdd5-5860-50ce-b445-12c73919d32f" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272346 1074 "To find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use the formula for differentiating parametric curves:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}\n$$\n\nFirst, we recall that to convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $r=12,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=12\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=12\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating the necessary derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\theta ) & =-12\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )=12\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{12\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{-12\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we arrive at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{\\theta =2\\pi /3} & =-\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =-(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272347 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271563 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272347 1069 "q-142389" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272347 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272347 1071 "Which of the following is equivalent to \n${\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}?$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272347 1072 #uuid "693a32b1-39ba-5f91-9f8b-21a365f10248" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272347 1074 "This is an improper integral of the second kind because the function under the integral is unbounded as we approach the *lower* integration limit from any point that lies in the interval $x\\in (1,4):$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}\\to \\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to {1}^{+}\n$$\n\nWe can attempt to evaluate this integral by integrating $f(x)$ over the interval $x\\in [a,4]$ and taking the limit as $a\\to {1}^{+}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to {1}^{+}}({\\int }_{a}^{4}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272348 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271718 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272348 1069 "q-142390" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272348 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272348 1071 "Which of the following is equivalent to \n${\\int }_{-1}^{5}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\sqrt{25-{x}^{2}}}?$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272348 1072 #uuid "b862b916-7ee1-544a-a853-572fec97fa2d" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272348 1074 "This is an improper integral of the second kind because the function under the integral is unbounded as we approach the *upper* integration limit from any point that lies in the interval $x\\in (-1,5):$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{25-{x}^{2}}}\\to \\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to {5}^{-}\n$$\n\nWe can attempt to evaluate this integral by integrating $f(x)$ over the interval $x\\in [-1,b]$ and taking the limit as $b\\to {5}^{-}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{5}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{25-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{b\\to {5}^{-}}({\\int }_{-1}^{b}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{25-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272349 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271589 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272349 1069 "q-142436" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272349 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272349 1071 "![](images/c7/c7fd7d815bd006c27be4fc793e4cdc8c66ccd119d86167ad7f7cb1da64a4f2bd.png)\n\nFind $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for the polar curve $r=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ shown above." 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272349 1072 #uuid "7d5f1ae2-d446-5455-83a4-5e182e77c947" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272349 1074 "To find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use the formula for differentiating parametric curves:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}\n$$\n\nFirst, we recall that to convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $r=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta , \\\\ y & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =3{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we used the identity $\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$\n\nTo compute our derivative, we first need to calculate ${x}^{′}(\\theta )$ and ${y}^{′}(\\theta ):$ \n\n- First, we compute the derivative of $x$ with respect to $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\theta ) & ={(\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta )}^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\cdot 2 \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute the derivative of $y$ with respect to $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\theta ) & =(3{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )^{′} \\\\  & =3\\cdot 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta } \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡2\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272350 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271775 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272350 1069 "q-142437" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272350 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272350 1071 "![](images/5b/5bbb314322763dad82b0390e9d9713ab551de16208a2eb0b75c4ceb3c1f9c611.png)\n\nFind $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for the polar curve $r=-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ shown above." 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272350 1072 #uuid "3757b821-40d9-55f7-879b-44232e8631c1" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272350 1074 "To find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use the formula for differentiating parametric curves:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}\n$$\n\nFirst, we recall that to convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $r=-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =-5{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =-\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we used the identity $\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$\n\nTo compute our derivative, we first need to calculate ${x}^{′}(\\theta )$ and ${y}^{′}(\\theta ):$ \n\n- First, we compute the derivative of $x$ with respect to $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\theta ) & =(-5{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )^{′} \\\\  & =-10\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =10\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute the derivative of $y$ with respect to $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\theta ) & ={(-\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta )}^{′} \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\cdot 2) \\\\  & =-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }{5\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta } \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cot}⁡2\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272351 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271906 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272351 1069 "q-14246" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272351 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272351 1071 "Find the general solution to the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=y{e}^{x}$ for $y\\ne 0.$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272351 1072 #uuid "c8ed818f-2b3f-54cd-b6b2-99b0955850e8" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272351 1074 "To find the general solution, we first separate the variables: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =y{e}^{x} \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\cancel{\\text{d}x}}\\,\\cancel{\\text{d}x} & =\\int {e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{y}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int {e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|y| & ={e}^{x}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation (in implicit form) is\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡|y|={e}^{x}+C\n$$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272352 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271940 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272352 1069 "q-14247" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272352 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272352 1071 "Find the general solution to the differential equation ${y}^{′}=y+3y{x}^{2}$ for $y\\ne 0.$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272352 1072 #uuid "8f251269-fa35-5ece-9af4-5538cedc8207" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272352 1074 "To find the general solution, we first separate the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y+3y{x}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =y+3y{x}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =y(1+3{x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =1+3{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (1+3{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{y}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int (1+3{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|y| & =x+{x}^{3}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation (in implicit form) is\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡|y|={x}^{3}+x+C.\n$$\n\nWe're required to write the solution in the form $y=y(x).$ By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|y| & ={x}^{3}+x+C \\\\ |y| & ={e}^{{x}^{3}+x+C} \\\\ |y| & ={e}^{{x}^{3}+x}\\cdot {e}^{C} \\\\ |y| & =K{e}^{{x}^{3}+x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K={e}^{C}.$\n\nWe can drop the absolute value bars by allowing $K$ to be either positive or negative. Therefore, the most general solution is\n\n$$\ny=K{e}^{{x}^{3}+x}.\n$$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272353 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271932 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272353 1069 "q-142474" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272353 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272353 1071 "Which of the following statements are true, given that $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix and \n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(A)={[\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ -{x}_{1}\\end{aligned}]\\,|\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in R}?\n$$\n\n- $\\text{dim(Null}(A))=1$\n- $A$ is invertible\n- $\\text{rank}(A)<4$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272353 1072 #uuid "4927fd87-c83e-52d3-b9bb-877110a8c514" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272353 1074 "Since $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{4} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=4=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, since \n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(A)={{x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,|\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in R}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that \n\n$$\n\\text{dim(Null}(A))=2.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $\\text{dim(Null}(A))\\ne 0,$ then according to the invertible matrix theorem, the matrix $A$ can't be invertible.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since $A$ is not invertible, then by the invertible matrix theorem, we must have $\\text{rank}(A)\\ne 4,$ or equivalently $\\text{rank}(A)<4.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272354 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271540 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272354 1069 "q-142475" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272354 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272354 1071 "Consider the following sentence:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\text{nullity}(A)=0,$ then  $\\text{__________}\\,.$*\n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n\n- $A$ is invertible\n- $\\text{rank}(A)<n$\n- $\\text{Col}(A)={R}^{n}$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272354 1072 #uuid "795129ab-7204-5692-a97c-e32525213e2a" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272354 1074 "The invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{n} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=n=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the options separately.\n\n- The following statement is true:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\text{nullity}(A)=0,$ then $A$ is invertible.* $\\,✓$ \n\nIndeed, according to the invertible matrix theorem, \n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,\\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A).$\n\n- The following statement is false:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\text{nullity}(A)=0,$ then $\\text{rank}(A)<n.$* $\\,\\times $ \n\nIndeed, since $A$ is invertible, then according to the invertible matrix theorem, we have $\\text{rank}(A)=n.$\n\n- The following statement is true:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\text{nullity}(A)=0,$ then $\\text{Col}(A)={R}^{n}.$*  $\\,✓$ \n\nIndeed, since $A$ is invertible, then according to the invertible matrix theorem, we have $\\text{Col}(A)={R}^{n}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272355 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271834 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272355 1069 "q-142476" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272355 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272355 1071 "Consider the following sentence:\n\n*If $A$ is a $8\\times 8$ matrix and $A$ is invertible,  then $\\text{__________}$ .*\n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n\n- $det(A)=0$\n- $\\text{Col}(A)={R}^{16}$\n- $A$ has $8$ pivot columns" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272355 1072 #uuid "7036dc73-9ca4-59f2-b983-11eaacfb5a4b" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272355 1074 "If $A$ is an $8\\times 8$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{8} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=8=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the options separately.\n\n- The following statement is false:\n\n*If $A$ is a $8\\times 8$ matrix and $A$ is invertible,  then $det(A)=0$* $\\,\\times $  \n\nSince the matrix $A$ is invertible, it must be  $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\n- The following statement is false:\n\n*If $A$ is a $8\\times 8$ matrix and $A$ is invertible,  then  $\\text{Col}(A)={R}^{16}.$*  $\\,\\times $\n\nIndeed, since $A$ is invertible, then according to the invertible matrix theorem,  we have $\\text{Col}(A)={R}^{8}.$\n\n- The following statement is true:\n\n*If $A$ is a $8\\times 8$ matrix and $A$ is invertible,  then $A$ has $8$ pivot columns.* $\\,✓$ \n\nSince $A$ is invertible, then according to the invertible matrix theorem, we have $\\text{dim(Col}(A))=8,$ which means that $A$ has exactly $8$ pivot columns.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272356 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271583 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272356 1069 "q-142480" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272356 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272356 1071 "Consider a $5\\times 6$ matrix $A.$ If $\\text{dim(Null(}A))=2,$ then $\\text{rank}(A)=$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272356 1072 #uuid "c3ee4195-fbf7-5133-aa9f-68dff638a011" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272356 1074 "According to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A)=n.\n$$\n\nFor the given matrix, we have $n=6.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ \\text{rank}(A)+2 & =6 \\\\ \\text{rank}(A) & =6-2 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272357 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271796 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272357 1069 "q-142486" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272357 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272357 1071 "Find $\\text{rank}(A)$ given that $\\text{Null}(A)={[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}-{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{1}-{x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]\\,|\\,\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}\\in R}.$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272357 1072 #uuid "c5556444-b76f-5774-b94f-93b8f2189ca9" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272357 1074 "According to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A)=n.\n$$\n\nFor the given matrix, we have $n=4,$ since this is the number of components in each of the vectors of the null space. \n\nAlso, since \n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(A)={{x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\,|\\,\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}\\in R}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $\\text{nullity}(A)=3.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ \\text{rank}(A)+3 & =4 \\\\ \\text{rank}(A) & =4-3 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272358 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271682 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272358 1069 "q-142487" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272358 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272358 1071 "Let $A$ be a $6\\times 8$ matrix. If $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3},{b}_{4},{b}_{5}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(A)$, find $\\text{dim(Null}(A)).$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272358 1072 #uuid "7d182fc6-16c7-589f-980f-eb790356faa5" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272358 1074 "According to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A)=n.\n$$\n\nFor the given matrix, we have $n=8,$ and since $B$ is formed by $5$ vectors, $\\text{rank}(A)=5.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ 5+\\text{nullity}(A) & =8 \\\\ \\text{nullity}(A) & =8-5 \\\\ \\text{dim(Null}(A)) & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272359 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271907 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272359 1069 "q-14253" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272359 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272359 1071 "Find the general solution to the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={y}^{2}+2{y}^{2}x$ for $y\\ne 0.$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272359 1072 #uuid "de6a882e-022d-59c5-adf8-2e2394047b45" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272359 1074 "To find the general solution, we first separate the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={y}^{2}+2{y}^{2}x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={y}^{2}(1+2x) \\\\ \\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =1+2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (1+2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int (1+2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ -\\frac{1}{y} & =x+{x}^{2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation (in implicit form) is\n\n$$\n-\\frac{1}{y}={x}^{2}+x+C.\n$$\n\nWe're required to write the solution in the form $y=y(x).$ By doing this, we get\n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+x+C}.\n$$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272360 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271896 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272360 1069 "q-142544" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272360 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272360 1071 "Which of the following statements are true given that $A$ is a $7\\times 7$ matrix which has only $3$ non-pivot column?\n\n- $A$ is  **not** invertible\n- $\\text{Col}(A)$ is a $4$-dimensional subspace of ${R}^{7}.$\n- $\\text{nullity}(A)=1$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272360 1072 #uuid "9a4ded73-dd50-5431-9dd3-a7f4831d94c0" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272360 1074 "Since $A$ is a $7\\times 7$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{7} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=7=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A)=n.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Since $A$ has $n=7$ columns and $3$ of them is a non-pivot one, it follows that    \n\n$$\n\\text{dim(Col}(A))=7-3=4\\ne 7.\n$$\n\n Thus,  according to the invertible matrix theorem $A$ is  **not** an invertible matrix.\n\n- Statement II is true.  Indeed, since $\\text{dim(Col}(A))=\\text{rank}(A)=4$,  then $\\text{Col}(A)$ is a $4$-dimensional subspace of ${R}^{7}.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $n=7$, according to  the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ 4+\\text{nullity}(A) & =7 \\\\ \\text{nullity}(A) & =7-4 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272361 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271528 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272361 1069 "q-142552" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272361 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272361 1071 "Given the joint probability distribution $f(x,y)$ below for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y,$ find $P(Y=2).$\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.1$ | $0.4$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.2$ | $0.3$ |" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272361 1072 #uuid "7fed86ff-5d99-5bec-a1c6-925c887eaecd" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272361 1074 "If the random variables $X$ and $Y$ have joint distribution with joint probability mass function $f(x,y),$ then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & ={f}_{X}(x)={\\sum }_{y}f(x,y) \\\\ P(Y=y) & ={f}_{Y}(y)={\\sum }_{x}f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal mass functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nIn this case, the marginal distribution for $X$ corresponds to the row totals, and the marginal distribution for $Y$ corresponds to the column totals. Let's add these row and column totals to our table:\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ | ${f}_{X}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.1$ | $0.4$ | $0.5$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.2$ | $0.3$ | $0.5$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}$ | $0.3$ | $0.7$ | $1$ |\n\nFinally, using the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y=2) & ={f}_{Y}(2) \\\\  & =f(1,2)+f(1,2) \\\\  & =0.4+0.3 \\\\  & =0.7.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272362 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271702 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272362 1069 "q-142557" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272362 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272362 1071 "Given the joint probability distribution $f(x,y)$ below for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y,$ find ${f}_{Y}(2).$\n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.15$ | $0.25$ | $0.2$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.25$ | $0.1$ | $0.05$ |" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272362 1072 #uuid "81d1721a-cc20-5d10-aa30-7f257189469f" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272362 1074 "If the random variables $X$ and $Y$ have joint distribution with joint probability mass function $f(x,y),$ then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & ={f}_{X}(x)={\\sum }_{y}f(x,y) \\\\ P(Y=y) & ={f}_{Y}(y)={\\sum }_{x}f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal mass functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nIn this case, the marginal distribution for $X$ corresponds to the row totals, and the marginal distribution for $Y$ corresponds to the column totals. Let's add these row and column totals to our table:\n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ | ${f}_{X}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.15$ | $0.25$ | $0.2$ | $0.6$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.25$ | $0.1$ | $0.05$ | $0.4$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}$ | $0.4$ | $0.35$ | $0.25$ | $1$ |\n\nFinally, using the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(2) & =f(0,2)+f(1,2) \\\\  & =0.2+0.05 \\\\  & =0.25.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272363 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271611 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272363 1069 "q-142559" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272363 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272363 1071 "Given the joint probability distribution $f(x,y)$ below for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y,$ find $P(Y=1).$\n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $\\frac{1}{16}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |\n| $X=2$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ | $\\frac{3}{16}$ |" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272363 1072 #uuid "59735bc3-a414-5af3-be99-af79ebdc3032" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272363 1074 "If the random variables $X$ and $Y$ have joint distribution with joint probability mass function $f(x,y),$ then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & ={f}_{X}(x)={\\sum }_{y}f(x,y) \\\\ P(Y=y) & ={f}_{Y}(y)={\\sum }_{x}f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal mass functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nIn this case, the marginal distribution for $X$ corresponds to the row totals, and the marginal distribution for $Y$ corresponds to the column totals. Let's add these row and column totals to our table:\n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ | ${f}_{X}$ |\n| --- | -