ing the secant-tangent identity as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2\\,{sec}^{2}⁡x-1}{1+2{tan}^{2}⁡x} & =\\frac{2\\,(1+{tan}^{2}⁡x)-1}{1+2{tan}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{2+2{tan}^{2}⁡x-1}{1+2{tan}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1+2{tan}^{2}⁡x}{1+2{tan}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{1+2{tan}^{2}⁡x}}{\\cancel{1+2{tan}^{2}⁡x}} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{2{sec}^{2}⁡x-1}{1+2{tan}^{2}⁡x}=1.\n$$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272385 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271642 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272385 1069 "q-142837" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272385 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272385 1071 "| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.1$ | $0.3$ | $0.05$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.2$ | $0.05$ | $0.1$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.05$ | $0.1$ | $0.05$ |\n\nGiven the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ above for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y,$ find the marginal mass function for $Y.$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272385 1072 #uuid "48d89cb8-c821-564e-bec6-26453ecdfd86" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272385 1074 "If the random variables $X$ and $Y$ have joint distribution with joint probability mass function $f(x,y),$ then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & ={f}_{X}(x)={\\sum }_{y}f(x,y) \\\\ P(Y=y) & ={f}_{Y}(y)={\\sum }_{x}f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal mass functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nIn this case, the marginal distribution for $X$ corresponds to the row totals, and the marginal distribution for $Y$ corresponds to the column totals. Let's add these row and column totals to our table:\n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ | ${f}_{X}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.1$ | $0.3$ | $0.05$ | $0.45$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.2$ | $0.05$ | $0.1$ | $0.35$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.05$ | $0.1$ | $0.05$ | $0.2$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}$ | $0.35$ | $0.45$ | $0.2$ | $1$ |\n\nFinally, the marginal mass function for $Y$ is given by the following table:\n\n| $y$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{Y}(y)$ | $0.35$ | $0.45$ | $0.2$ |" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272386 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271959 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272386 1069 "q-142839" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272386 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272386 1071 "$\\frac{1-{sec}^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}=$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272386 1072 #uuid "c7824f8f-c1b4-50c2-a8cc-85b1111053c3" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272386 1074 "First, let's recall the secant-tangent identity:\n\n$$\n1+{tan}^{2}⁡x={sec}^{2}⁡x\n$$\n\nWe rewrite the given expression using the secant-tangent identity as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1-{sec}^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x} & =\\frac{1-(1+{tan}^{2}⁡x)}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{-{tan}^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\cdot \\operatorname{tan}⁡x}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\cdot \\cancel{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{1-{sec}^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}=-\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.\n$$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272387 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271745 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272387 1069 "q-142842" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272387 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272387 1071 "$\\frac{{tan}^{3}⁡\\theta }{1-{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta }=$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272387 1072 #uuid "144c40a8-184c-5b0c-9d8c-a01781e4fbf6" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272387 1074 "First, let's recall the secant-tangent identity:\n\n$$\n1+{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta ={sec}^{2}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe rewrite the given expression using the secant-tangent identity as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{tan}^{3}⁡\\theta }{1-{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta } & =\\frac{{tan}^{3}⁡\\theta }{1-(1+{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{{tan}^{3}⁡\\theta }{1-1-{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =-\\frac{{tan}^{3}⁡\\theta }{{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =-\\frac{{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta }{{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\cancel{{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta }{\\cancel{{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta }} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{{tan}^{3}⁡\\theta }{1-{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta }=-\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta .\n$$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272388 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271856 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272388 1069 "q-142871" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272388 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272388 1071 "${cos}^{2}⁡x\\text{ }\\,({sec}^{2}⁡x-2{tan}^{2}⁡x)=$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272388 1072 #uuid "fc757669-b204-5044-83d4-679ef2bca4ac" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272388 1074 "First, let's recall the secant-tangent identity:\n\n$$\n1+{tan}^{2}⁡x={sec}^{2}⁡x\n$$\n\nWe rewrite the given expression using the secant-tangent identity as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡x\\text{ }\\,({sec}^{2}⁡x-2{tan}^{2}⁡x) & ={cos}^{2}⁡x\\text{ }\\,(1+{tan}^{2}⁡x-2{tan}^{2}⁡x) \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡x(1-{tan}^{2}⁡x) \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡x-{cos}^{2}⁡x\\cdot {tan}^{2}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we recall the identities:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x},\\,{cos}^{2}⁡x=1-{sin}^{2}⁡x\n$$\n\n \n\nUsing the above identities, we can further simplify our expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡x-{cos}^{2}⁡x\\cdot {tan}^{2}⁡x & ={cos}^{2}⁡x-{cos}^{2}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡x}{{cos}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡x-\\cancel{{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡x}{\\cancel{{cos}^{2}⁡x}} \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡x-{sin}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡x-{sin}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =1-2{sin}^{2}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{cos}^{2}⁡x\\text{ }\\,({sec}^{2}⁡x-2{tan}^{2}⁡x)=1-2{sin}^{2}⁡x.\n$$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272389 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271513 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272389 1069 "q-142878" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272389 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272389 1071 "$(1-{sec}^{2}\\,x)\\cdot {cos}^{2}⁡x=$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272389 1072 #uuid "e721a691-c57d-59d2-9089-3c455511ef00" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272389 1074 "First, let's recall the secant-tangent identity:\n\n$$\n1+{tan}^{2}⁡x={sec}^{2}⁡x\n$$\n\nWe rewrite the given expression using the secant-tangent identity as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(1-{sec}^{2}⁡x)\\cdot {cos}^{2}⁡x & =(1-(1+{tan}^{2}⁡x))\\cdot {cos}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =-{tan}^{2}⁡x\\cdot {cos}^{2}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we recall the following identity:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\n$$\n\n \n\nUsing the above identity, we can further simplify our expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{tan}^{2}⁡x\\cdot {cos}^{2}⁡x & =-\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡x}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\cdot {cos}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =-\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡x}{\\cancel{{cos}^{2}⁡x}}\\cdot \\cancel{{cos}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =-{sin}^{2}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n(1-{sec}^{2}\\,x)\\cdot {cos}^{2}⁡x=-{sin}^{2}⁡x.\n$$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272390 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271721 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272390 1069 "q-142950" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272390 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272390 1071 "| $f$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.1$ | $0.3$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.2$ | $0.4$ |\n\nFind the conditional probability mass function ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,2)$ given that the random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above." 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272390 1072 #uuid "d0f4e8ee-b7fc-5bf2-8157-bde25fb332d5" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272390 1074 "The conditional probability mass function ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)=P(Y=y\\,|\\,X=x).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we're required to compute the following set of conditional probabilities:\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,2)=P(Y=y\\,|\\,X=2)\n$$\n\nBy the multiplication law for conditional probability, we have\n\n$$\nP(Y=y\\,|\\,X=2)=\\frac{P(X=2,Y=y)}{P(X=2)}=\\frac{f(2,y)}{{f}_{X}(2)}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability mass function of $X$ and $Y,$ and ${f}_{X}(x)$ is the marginal distribution of $X.$\n\nRecall that the marginal distributions for $X$ and $Y$ correspond to the row and column totals, respectively. So, let's compute these:\n\n| $f$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=4$ | ${f}_{X}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.1$ | $0.3$ | $0.4$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.2$ | $0.4$ | $0.6$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}(y)$ | $0.3$ | $0.7$ | $1$ |\n\nTherefore, for $y\\in {2,4},$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=2\\,|\\,Y=2) & =\\frac{f(2,2)}{{f}_{X}(2)}=\\frac{0.2}{0.6}=\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ P(X=2\\,|\\,Y=4) & =\\frac{f(2,4)}{{f}_{X}(2)}=\\frac{0.4}{0.6}=\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the conditional probability mass function can be represented by the following table:   \n\n| $y$ | $2$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,2)$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ | $\\frac{2}{3}$ |" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272391 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271809 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272391 1069 "q-142977" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272391 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272391 1071 "![](images/b1/b12b053e195f242bdf15499ee38f9b00830cd9d2a51e17a7df15ccf427b9f9c2.png)\n\nConsider the diagram shown above, where $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}=a,$ $\\overset{\\to }{QT}=b,$ and $\\overset{\\to }{PS}=c.$ Find $\\overset{\\to }{PT}$ in terms of $a,b,$ and $c.$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272391 1072 #uuid "bf47c89c-c054-559c-9171-d57b2e925b15" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272391 1074 "We want to find a \"path\" from $P$ to $T$ consisting only of the vectors $a,b,$ and $c.$\n\nAccording to the triangle law of addition,\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{PT}=\\overset{\\to }{PQ}+\\overset{\\to }{QT},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/f5/f5a772ed6d71bb8654fc4fc1ad6be444c6c12827b5d582cec1af18b3329ecfb9.png)\n\n \n\nNow, from the original diagram, we have that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{PQ}=a,\\,\\overset{\\to }{QT}=b.\n$$\n\nLet's add these to our diagram.\n\n![](images/ad/ad3812200ad6af06a69d8f105c8161b7a772512a5c2ad07e12756256663f20a6.png)\n\n \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PT} & =\\overset{\\to }{PQ}+\\overset{\\to }{QT} \\\\  & =a+b\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272392 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271647 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272392 1069 "q-142998" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272392 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272392 1071 "![](images/5c/5c7723e43696fac78180663115c50ed76c81c8841e3a99eb26122860f798dcc4.png)\n\nConsider the diagram shown above, where $\\overset{\\to }{QT}=a,$ $\\overset{\\to }{RS}=b,$ $\\overset{\\to }{TR}=c,$ and $\\overset{\\to }{RP}=d.$ Find $\\overset{\\to }{QS}$ in terms of $a,b,c,$ and $d.$" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272392 1072 #uuid "c988a779-621b-5247-8a3d-1aac60b5b514" 13194139533402 17592186045585 true] [17592186272392 1074 "We want to find a \"path\" from $Q$ to $S$ consisting only of the vectors $a,b,c,$ and $d.$\n\nAccording to the triangle law of addition,\n\n$$\n\\ove